const PHYSICS_D = [
{
 id:'cosmo', n:'Cosmology (ΛCDM)', aka:'Friedmann–Lemaître–Robertson–Walker · the concordance model',
 dom:'physics', tier:3, years:'1917 · 1922–1927 · 1998 –', status:'well-confirmed',
 scale:{len:'10²² – 10²⁷ m', en:'10⁻⁴ eV – 10¹⁰ GeV', lg:9},
 one:'General relativity applied to the whole universe under an assumed symmetry, fitted with six numbers, four of which describe substances nobody has identified in a laboratory — and which nevertheless reproduces the microwave sky, the light-element abundances and the galaxy distribution at once.',
 onto:'One spacetime with a preferred foliation by surfaces of constant cosmic time; on it, four smoothly distributed components — photons and neutrinos, baryons, cold dark matter, and a constant vacuum energy Λ — plus a field of small Gaussian perturbations laid down before the earliest epoch the theory describes. The ontology is frankly incomplete: 95% of the energy budget is named after its gravitational effect rather than its constitution, and "dark matter" and "dark energy" are placeholders whose contents are the open question, not a settled inventory.',
 state:'A scale factor a(t) with a(t₀)=1, a spatial curvature constant k, and a set of density parameters Ω_i summing (with curvature) to one; plus, at linear order, the Fourier amplitudes of the perturbation fields δ(k,t), Θ_ℓ(k,t), Φ(k,t), evolved by the Einstein–Boltzmann hierarchy. The background state is six numbers; the perturbed state is a set of transfer functions plus a primordial power spectrum specified by two more.',

 axioms:[
  {n:'The cosmological principle',
   s:R`\text{On scales } \gtrsim 100\ h^{-1}\,\mathrm{Mpc},\ \text{spacetime is spatially homogeneous and isotropic.}`,
   t:'The symmetry assumption that reduces the ten field equations to two ordinary differential equations. Isotropy about us is measured — the CMB is isotropic to 10⁻⁵ after the dipole is removed. Homogeneity is not measured; it is inferred by adding the Copernican principle, that we occupy no special position. Ellis has pressed hardest on the point that this extra step is a metaphysical posit disguised as an observation.'},
  {n:'Weyl\'s postulate',
   s:R`\text{Matter worldlines form a non-intersecting geodesic congruence orthogonal to a family of spacelike hypersurfaces.}`,
   t:'What licenses a global cosmic time coordinate at all. Without it the notion of "the age of the universe" or "the temperature now" has no referent, since general relativity supplies no preferred slicing. Weyl introduced it in 1923; it is logically independent of the cosmological principle, and is violated wherever structures have collapsed and crossed.'},
  {n:'Einstein\'s equations with a cosmological constant',
   s:R`G_{\mu\nu}+\Lambda g_{\mu\nu}=\frac{8\pi G}{c^{4}}T_{\mu\nu}`,
   t:'Λ is not derived from anything. Lovelock\'s theorem shows it is the only extra term allowed in four dimensions by second-order, divergence-free, generally covariant field equations — so its presence is unsurprising; its *value* is what nothing explains. Attempts to derive it from vacuum energy give an answer wrong by 10⁵⁵ to 10¹²⁰.'},
  {n:'Perfect-fluid matter with barotropic equations of state',
   s:R`T^{\mu}{}_{\nu}=\mathrm{diag}(-\rho c^{2},p,p,p),\qquad p=w\rho c^{2},\ \ w\in\{0,\tfrac13,-1\}`,
   t:'Each component is idealised as a single fluid with no shear, no viscosity and no anisotropic stress. The assumption is what makes the Friedmann equations closed. It is exactly true for a homogeneous isotropic distribution and false at the percent level for free-streaming neutrinos, which do carry anisotropic stress and must be handled with a Boltzmann hierarchy instead.'},
  {n:'Primordial perturbations are adiabatic, Gaussian and nearly scale-invariant',
   s:R`\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{R}}(k)=A_{s}\left(\frac{k}{k_{*}}\right)^{n_{s}-1},\qquad n_{s}=0.9649\pm0.0042`,
   t:'Two numbers replace an entire unknown history. Inflation is the usual supplier of this postulate, but the postulate is logically prior and can be adopted without it: one may simply assert the initial spectrum and fit. Isocurvature admixtures are bounded below a few percent by Planck, and the strongest test of Gaussianity gives f_NL^local = −0.9 ± 5.1.'},
  {n:'Local physics is universal',
   s:R`\alpha,\ G,\ m_{e}/m_{p}\ \text{take their laboratory values at all }z\ \text{and all positions.}`,
   t:'Rarely stated and always used. Quasar absorption spectra constrain Δα/α below ~10⁻⁵ back to z ≈ 4; BBN and the CMB constrain any variation in G to a few percent over 13 Gyr. Dropping it dissolves most of the evidence, since every distance and every abundance is read off an assumed atomic physics.'}
 ],

 hyps:[
  {h:'Most of the matter is cold, collisionless, non-baryonic and stable over a Hubble time.',
   k:'core',
   b:'Forced by three independent facts: cluster and galaxy dynamics, the ratio of the odd to even acoustic peaks in the CMB, and the growth of structure from 10⁻⁵ to order unity in 13 Gyr, which baryons alone cannot do because they are coupled to photons until recombination. If dark matter is not a particle, essentially every quantitative success of the model has to be re-derived from something else. Direct searches have returned nothing: LZ excludes spin-independent cross-sections above ~10⁻⁴⁷ cm² near 30 GeV.'},
  {h:'The accelerating component is a cosmological constant with w = −1 exactly and no spatial fluctuations.',
   k:'core',
   b:'Written into the name. The data currently permit w = −1.03 ± 0.03 from CMB+BAO+SNe; the DESI baryon-acoustic measurements of 2024–25 prefer an evolving w₀w_a model at between 2.6σ and 4.2σ depending on which supernova compilation is combined, which is the first serious quantitative pressure on this hypothesis. If it falls, ΛCDM is replaced rather than repaired.'},
  {h:'A period of accelerated expansion preceded the hot phase and set the initial conditions.',
   k:'auxiliary',
   b:'Introduced by Guth in 1981 to solve the horizon, flatness and monopole problems. It is auxiliary in the strict Duhemian sense: ΛCDM parameter fitting proceeds unchanged if one simply postulates the initial spectrum. Its predictive content is contested — flatness and near-scale-invariance were stated in advance, but the class of inflationary models is large enough to accommodate almost any n_s and r, which is Steinhardt, Ijjas and Loeb\'s complaint against Guth, Linde, Kaiser and 30 co-signatories in 2017.'},
  {h:'General relativity is unmodified out to the horizon and back to z ≈ 10⁹.',
   k:'auxiliary',
   b:'Independently revisable, and the target of every modified-gravity programme from MOND through f(R) and TeVeS. GW170817 killed a large class of them in one stroke by fixing the gravitational-wave speed to |c_gw/c − 1| < 10⁻¹⁵. What survives generally does worse than cold dark matter on cluster scales and on the CMB peak structure while doing better on galaxy rotation curves — a split verdict that has not moved in thirty years.'},
  {h:'Type Ia supernovae, Cepheids, the BAO ruler and the sound horizon at last scattering measure distance.',
   k:'bridge',
   b:'The correspondence rules that convert photons into geometry. Each carries its own calibration chain, and the H₀ tension is exactly a disagreement between two such chains rather than between two theories. Note that the BAO and CMB rulers are both calibrated by the sound horizon r_d ≈ 147 Mpc, computed from the model itself — so they are not independent of it, a point that "early dark energy" proposals exploit.'},
  {h:'The universe is in a steady state, with matter continuously created to keep density constant.',
   k:'abandoned',
   b:'Bondi, Gold and Hoyle, 1948. Killed by radio-source counts in the 1950s–60s, by the existence of quasars only at high redshift, and decisively by the CMB blackbody, which a steady state has no natural way to thermalise. Its replacement is the hot big bang. It deserves to be remembered as a genuinely falsifiable rival that was falsified — which is more than can be said for several of its successors.'},
  {h:'Λ = 0; the expansion decelerates, and the only question is by how much.',
   k:'abandoned',
   b:'The working assumption of essentially all cosmology from Einstein\'s renunciation of Λ in 1931 until 1998. Sandage framed the whole enterprise as "a search for two numbers", H₀ and q₀, with q₀ > 0 taken for granted. The 1998 supernova results from the High-z and Supernova Cosmology Project teams found q₀ < 0, and Λ was reinstated — as a fitted number, not a derived one.'}
 ],

 preds:[
  {p:'Relic radiation from the hot phase survives as a blackbody at a few kelvin.',
   num:R`T_{0}=2.72548\pm0.00057\ \mathrm{K}`,
   st:'confirmed',
   b:'Alpher and Herman put it at about 5 K in 1948; Gamow gave varying figures. The prediction was then forgotten for seventeen years and played no part in the 1965 detection by Penzias and Wilson, who were chasing excess antenna noise. COBE/FIRAS in 1990 showed the spectrum is a blackbody to within 50 parts per million — the most precise blackbody ever measured, and a fact no steady-state model reproduces.'},
  {p:'The first acoustic peak in the CMB angular power spectrum sits at the angle subtended by the sound horizon; in a flat universe that is about one degree.',
   num:R`\ell_{1}=220.6\pm0.6,\qquad \Omega_{k}=0.0007\pm0.0019`,
   st:'confirmed',
   b:'The peak position was computed by Sunyaev and Zel\'dovich (1970) and Peebles and Yu (1970) before any anisotropy had been detected at all. BOOMERanG and MAXIMA measured ℓ₁ ≈ 200 in 2000; Planck fixed it to sub-percent precision. A peak at ℓ ≈ 300 would have meant an open universe and would have falsified the inflationary flatness claim outright.'},
  {p:'Primordial abundances of D, ³He, ⁴He and ⁷Li follow from a single parameter, the baryon-to-photon ratio.',
   num:R`\mathrm{D/H}=(2.527\pm0.030)\times10^{-5},\quad Y_{p}=0.245\pm0.003`,
   st:'confirmed',
   b:'The deuterium abundance measured in metal-poor damped Lyman-α systems fixes Ω_b h² = 0.0222 ± 0.0003, agreeing with the entirely independent CMB value 0.02237 ± 0.00015 to better than one sigma. Two measurements from different epochs, different physics and different instruments landing on the same number is the strongest single argument for the hot big bang.'},
  {p:'The same one-parameter network gives the primordial ⁷Li abundance.',
   num:R`^{7}\mathrm{Li/H}_{\rm pred}\approx5\times10^{-10}\ \text{vs}\ \approx1.6\times10^{-10}\ \text{observed}`,
   st:'failed',
   b:'A factor of three to four discrepancy, persistent since the Spite plateau was characterised in 1982 and not closed by thirty years of improved reaction rates. The standard defence is that surface lithium in old halo stars is depleted by gravitational settling and rotational mixing — a stellar-astrophysics auxiliary, not a cosmological one. That is a textbook Duhemian rescue, and it may well be correct.'},
  {p:'The baryon acoustic oscillation leaves a bump in the galaxy correlation function at the comoving sound horizon.',
   num:R`r_{d}\approx147.1\pm0.3\ \mathrm{Mpc}\ (\approx100\ h^{-1}\mathrm{Mpc})`,
   st:'confirmed',
   b:'Predicted from CMB physics alone, then found by Eisenstein and collaborators in 2005 in 46 748 SDSS luminous red galaxies at 3.4σ. It is now measured at sub-percent precision by DESI across 0.3 < z < 2.3. The prediction was quantitative and prior, and the feature is a single bump at a computed position — as clean a novel prediction as observational cosmology has produced.'},
  {p:'The present expansion is accelerating, driven by a component with negative pressure.',
   num:R`\Omega_{\Lambda}=0.6847\pm0.0073,\qquad q_{0}\approx-0.53`,
   st:'retrodiction',
   b:'This must be stated plainly: acceleration was *discovered*, not predicted. Both 1998 teams expected to measure a deceleration parameter. Λ was then restored to the model and its value fitted to the data it was introduced to explain. The subsequent agreement of CMB, BAO and cluster-abundance determinations of Ω_Λ is real independent support, but the original move was accommodation.'},
  {p:'Inflation generated a background of primordial gravitational waves imprinted as B-mode polarisation.',
   num:R`r<0.036\ (95\%\ \mathrm{CL})`,
   st:'open',
   b:'The one prediction that could still discriminate sharply. BICEP2 claimed a detection at r ≈ 0.2 in March 2014 and withdrew it within the year when Planck showed the signal was polarised Galactic dust. BICEP/Keck plus Planck now bound r < 0.036. CMB-S4 and LiteBIRD target σ(r) ≈ 10⁻³, which would test the simplest large-field models; a null result there does not kill inflation, which is part of the complaint against it.'},
  {p:'Locally measured and CMB-inferred expansion rates agree.',
   num:R`H_{0}=73.04\pm1.04\ \text{vs}\ 67.36\pm0.54\ \mathrm{km\,s^{-1}Mpc^{-1}}`,
   st:'failed',
   b:'A 5σ discrepancy as of the SH0ES 2022 analysis. The Carnegie–Chicago programme using tip-of-the-red-giant-branch calibration finds ≈ 69.8 ± 1.9 and does not see the tension as established; JWST Cepheid photometry since 2023 has been read both ways. Either a calibration systematic hides in the distance ladder, or the sound horizon is shorter than ΛCDM says, which requires new physics before recombination.'}
 ],

 eqs:[
  {tex:R`ds^{2}=-c^{2}dt^{2}+a^{2}(t)\left[\frac{dr^{2}}{1-kr^{2}}+r^{2}d\Omega^{2}\right]`, name:'FRW line element',
   gloss:'The unique metric compatible with homogeneity and isotropy, up to the discrete choice k ∈ {−1,0,+1} and the function a(t). Note what this does: it removes all dynamics except one function of one variable before any physics is applied. The redshift 1+z = 1/a is then a geometric fact, not a Doppler shift — galaxies are not moving through space in this picture.'},
  {tex:R`\left(\frac{\dot a}{a}\right)^{2}=\frac{8\pi G}{3}\rho-\frac{kc^{2}}{a^{2}}+\frac{\Lambda c^{2}}{3}`, name:'Friedmann equation',
   gloss:'The 00 component of the field equations under the FRW ansatz. It is a constraint, not an evolution equation — which is why the curvature term acts like a fluid with w = −1/3 and Λ like one with w = −1. Written as Σ Ω_i = 1 it becomes the bookkeeping identity every parameter table reports.'},
  {tex:R`\frac{\ddot a}{a}=-\frac{4\pi G}{3}\left(\rho+\frac{3p}{c^{2}}\right)+\frac{\Lambda c^{2}}{3}`, name:'Acceleration (Raychaudhuri) equation',
   gloss:'Gravity responds to ρ + 3p/c², not to ρ. This is the whole content of "dark energy": anything with p < −ρc²/3 makes the expansion accelerate. It is also why pressure gravitates, a purely relativistic effect with no Newtonian analogue, and why a radiation-dominated universe decelerates faster than a matter-dominated one.'},
  {tex:R`H^{2}(z)=H_{0}^{2}\left[\Omega_{r}(1{+}z)^{4}+\Omega_{m}(1{+}z)^{3}+\Omega_{k}(1{+}z)^{2}+\Omega_{\Lambda}\right]`, name:'Expansion history',
   gloss:'The whole background model in one line. Each component dominates in turn: radiation until z ≈ 3400, matter until z ≈ 0.3, Λ thereafter. That we live within a factor of two of the matter–Λ crossover is the coincidence problem, and no dynamical explanation of it has survived.'},
  {tex:R`\rho_{\Lambda}=\frac{\Lambda c^{2}}{8\pi G}\approx5.9\times10^{-27}\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}\approx(2.25\ \mathrm{meV})^{4}`, name:'The measured value of Λ',
   gloss:'A fitted number, quoted here deliberately as a measurement rather than a prediction. Nothing in the theory constrains it. Naive quantum field theory estimates of the vacuum energy exceed it by 10¹²⁰ if cut off at the Planck scale and by 10⁵⁵ if cut off at the QCD scale — the worst quantitative failure in the history of physics, and it is a failure of the theories below, not of ΛCDM.'},
  {tex:R`\theta_{*}=\frac{r_{s}(z_{*})}{D_{A}(z_{*})},\qquad 100\,\theta_{*}=1.04109\pm0.00030`, name:'The acoustic angular scale',
   gloss:'The best-measured quantity in cosmology, at 0.03% — better than H₀ by two orders of magnitude. Everything else in the CMB fit is anchored to it. It is a ratio of a computed sound horizon to a computed distance, so its precision is the model\'s precision, not an unmediated observation: the H₀ tension can be rewritten entirely as a disagreement about r_s.'},
  {tex:R`\delta_{k}''+\mathcal{H}\delta_{k}'-4\pi G a^{2}\bar\rho\,\delta_{k}=0\ \Rightarrow\ \delta\propto a\ \ (\text{matter era})`, name:'Linear growth of perturbations',
   gloss:'Gravitational instability in an expanding background: growth is power-law, not exponential, because expansion drains it. This is why 10⁻⁵ fluctuations at recombination need cold dark matter to reach order unity today — baryons, held smooth by photon pressure until z ≈ 1100, would only reach 10⁻² and there would be no galaxies.'}
 ],
 concepts:[
  {t:'Comoving coordinates and the particle horizon', d:'Coordinates carried along by the expansion, so that comoving separations are constant for unperturbed matter. The particle horizon — about 46 Gly in comoving radius — is larger than c times the age because the intervening space expanded while light crossed it. Textbooks routinely claim nothing recedes faster than light; galaxies beyond z ≈ 1.5 have recession velocities above c and are perfectly visible, because recession velocity is not a relative velocity in any local sense.'},
  {t:'Dark matter as a placeholder', d:'The term names a role — gravitating, non-luminous, effectively pressureless, non-baryonic — not a substance. The evidence for the role is strong and multiply independent; the evidence for any particular occupant is nil. Candidate masses under active consideration span 10⁻²² eV (fuzzy dark matter) to 10 solar masses (primordial black holes), a range of about 90 orders of magnitude, which is an honest measure of how little is known.'},
  {t:'Dark energy and the coincidence problem', d:'Ω_Λ and Ω_m are within a factor of two today, though they scale as a⁰ and a⁻³ and so are equal for only about one e-fold out of the history. Either this is a selection effect on when observers exist, or it is a clue. Weinberg\'s 1987 anthropic bound — that Λ much larger would prevent structure from forming at all — is the only argument that produced roughly the right magnitude before the measurement, which is why it is taken more seriously than its critics would like.'},
  {t:'The acoustic peaks', d:'Before recombination, photons and baryons form a single fluid in which dark-matter potential wells drive standing sound waves. Modes caught at extrema when the coupling ends imprint a harmonic series. The odd/even peak asymmetry measures the baryon inertia and hence Ω_b; the third peak measures Ω_c; the damping tail measures the expansion rate at that epoch. Six numbers reproduce roughly 2500 multipoles.'},
  {t:'Inflation', d:'A hypothesised epoch of quasi-de Sitter expansion, at least ~60 e-folds, which makes the observed patch causally connected and flat and stretches quantum fluctuations to cosmological scales. Its successes are generic to the class rather than specific to any model, and its failures — the measure problem, the initial-conditions problem it was meant to solve reappearing as the question of how inflation itself starts — are structural. It is best described as a well-motivated framework awaiting a theory.'},
  {t:'The S₈ tension', d:'Weak-lensing surveys measure S₈ = σ₈√(Ω_m/0.3) around 0.76–0.78, against Planck\'s 0.832 ± 0.013. This was a 2–3σ discrepancy through the late 2010s; more recent joint DES–KiDS reanalyses have moved it toward agreement, and it is fair to say the tension has softened rather than been resolved. Unlike H₀, it can be absorbed by baryonic feedback in the small-scale matter power spectrum, which is not computed from first principles.'},
  {t:'Precision versus accuracy', d:'ΛCDM reports six parameters at sub-percent precision. That precision is conditional on the model: it is the width of a posterior within an assumed six-parameter family, not a bound on the truth. Every quoted error bar assumes the cosmological principle, Λ exactly constant, GR unmodified and the standard recombination history. This is the single most important caveat for a philosopher reading a cosmological parameter table.'}
 ],
 results:[
  {n:'The CMB blackbody (COBE/FIRAS, 1990)', s:'Deviations from a 2.725 K Planck spectrum below 50 parts per million — the spectrum was published with error bars smaller than the plotted line width.'},
  {n:'CMB anisotropy and its spectrum (COBE 1992 → Planck 2018)', s:'ΔT/T ≈ 10⁻⁵ first detected at 7° resolution; Planck now fits ℓ = 2–2500 in temperature and polarisation with six free parameters and χ²/dof near unity.'},
  {n:'Concordance of independent Ω_b determinations', s:'BBN deuterium gives Ω_b h² = 0.0222 ± 0.0003; the CMB acoustic peaks give 0.02237 ± 0.00015. Different epochs, different physics, same number.'},
  {n:'Accelerating expansion (1998)', s:'High-z Supernova Search and Supernova Cosmology Project, ~50 SNe Ia, ~0.25 mag fainter at z ≈ 0.5 than a decelerating model allows. Nobel 2011.'},
  {n:'Structure formation in simulation', s:'N-body computations from Millennium onward reproduce the observed two-point correlation function and halo mass function across four decades in mass, with cold dark matter and Gaussian initial conditions as the only inputs.'},
  {n:'The Bullet Cluster (2006)', s:'In 1E 0657−558 the X-ray gas, which carries most of the baryonic mass, is spatially offset from the weak-lensing mass peaks by about 0.7 Mpc — hard to accommodate in any purely modified-gravity account.'},
  {n:'Age concordance', s:'The model gives 13.797 ± 0.023 Gyr; the oldest globular clusters date to 13.0 ± 0.4 Gyr. In the mid-1990s these two numbers were inconsistent, and the discrepancy was a serious argument against the then-standard model.'}
 ],
 valid:{
  holds:'From roughly one second after the hot phase begins (BBN) to the present, on comoving scales above ~10 Mpc where linear perturbation theory applies, and for redshifts up to z ≈ 10⁹. Within that domain it fits thousands of data points with six parameters.',
  breaks:'Below ~1 Mpc, where baryonic feedback and non-linear collapse dominate and the small-scale predictions (core–cusp, missing satellites, too-big-to-fail) remain disputed. Before ~10⁻² s, where the physics is extrapolated rather than tested. And at t → 0, where the singularity theorems apply and the theory predicts its own failure.'
 },
 detail:{
  deriv:[
   {h:'What the Friedmann equation is and is not a derivation of', b:'Start with the metric ansatz. Homogeneity and isotropy force the spatial slices to have constant curvature, so the metric is determined up to a(t) and k — this is a theorem (the FRW uniqueness result), not an approximation. Compute the Einstein tensor: the 00 component gives the Friedmann equation and the ii component the acceleration equation, of which only one is independent given the conservation law. So far this is exact general relativity, conditional on the symmetry. The deception enters at the next step. The symmetry is assumed to hold for the *actual* universe, which is manifestly lumpy, on the grounds that a(t) describes the average. But general relativity is non-linear, so the average of the field equations is not the field equations of the average: the difference is a set of backreaction terms. Whether they matter is a live technical question — Buchert has argued they can mimic Λ, Green and Wald have a theorem that under their assumptions the backreaction behaves as a traceless dust and cannot; the assumptions are themselves disputed. Standard practice assumes the backreaction is negligible, and this assumption is essentially never listed among the model\'s ingredients even though it is logically prior to all of them.'},
   {h:'Six parameters, and what they are not', b:'The base fit is {Ω_b h², Ω_c h², 100θ_*, τ, ln(10¹⁰A_s), n_s}. Note the choices. θ_* is used rather than H₀ because it is measured 30 times better and is less degenerate. Flatness is imposed, not fitted, so Ω_k does not appear; relaxing it costs a factor of several in the H₀ error. The neutrino mass sum is fixed at 0.06 eV, the minimum allowed by oscillations, because the data cannot constrain it independently of other parameters — a fixed value quietly doing work. The recombination history is computed by a code (RECFAST, CosmoRec) implementing a 300-level hydrogen atom, and a one-percent error there shifts H₀ by more than the quoted uncertainty. What is reported as "six-parameter cosmology" is a six-parameter fit inside a much larger apparatus of fixed choices, each of which a careful reader should count as an auxiliary hypothesis.'},
   {h:'Why the horizon problem is a problem', b:'At recombination the comoving particle horizon subtends about 1.7° on the sky in a purely decelerating model. Regions further apart than that have never been in causal contact, yet they have the same temperature to one part in 10⁵. This is not an inconsistency — one may simply say the initial conditions were uniform — but it makes uniformity an unexplained brute fact over roughly 10⁴ causally disconnected patches. Inflation removes it by making the patches causally connected before inflation began, at the cost of postulating a scalar field with a flat potential and an unexplained initial state. The philosophical structure here is worth noticing: the horizon problem is not an empirical anomaly but a dissatisfaction with a class of initial conditions, and whether that dissatisfaction is a legitimate scientific demand is precisely what Penrose, Earman and Mosterín have disputed. Earman and Mosterín (1999) argued the fine-tuning arguments assume a probability measure on initial conditions that nobody has supplied, and the measure problem remains open.'}
  ],
  deeper:[
   {h:'Is the cosmological principle testable?', b:'Isotropy about our worldline is measurable and measured. Homogeneity is not, because we see a single past light cone: what looks like distant structure is at earlier times, and separating spatial variation from temporal evolution requires a model. The Ehlers–Geren–Sachs theorem supplies the standard defence — if all freely falling observers see an exactly isotropic radiation field, spacetime is FRW — but its antecedent quantifies over observers we cannot consult, so it converts an observational claim into a Copernican one. The almost-EGS theorem of Stoeger, Maartens and Ellis weakens this to approximate isotropy and gets approximate FRW, which is better but inherits the same quantifier. Partial empirical checks exist: the kinetic Sunyaev–Zel\'dovich effect constrains how anisotropic the CMB looks from other locations, and radial BAO measurements test the distance–redshift relation against expectations. Sylos Labini and collaborators have argued from galaxy catalogues that a homogeneity scale is not cleanly reached even at 100 Mpc/h; most of the field disagrees. The honest verdict: the principle is partially testable, was adopted for its mathematical tractability, and has survived every test applied to it.'},
   {h:'Prediction, retrodiction and the inflationary dispute', b:'Inflation is defended as having predicted flatness, near-scale-invariance, adiabaticity, Gaussianity and superhorizon correlations. Take these one at a time. Flatness: stated in 1981, measured in 2000 — a genuine prior prediction, though one that a great many models share. Near-scale-invariance with n_s slightly below one: Mukhanov and Chibisov (1981) and Starobinsky derived a tilt before the measurement, but a Harrison–Zel\'dovich spectrum with n_s = 1 had been proposed on symmetry grounds in 1970 without inflation, so inflation predicted the deviation rather than the spectrum. Adiabaticity and Gaussianity: generic to single-field models, and their observation excludes multi-field and non-Gaussian variants rather than confirming the framework. Superhorizon correlations, visible as the TE anticorrelation near ℓ ≈ 150 that WMAP found, are the hardest to obtain otherwise and are the strongest item on the list. Against all this stands the measure problem: eternal inflation produces an infinite volume in which all outcomes occur, and no agreed regularisation makes any of them more probable. Steinhardt\'s charge is that inflation in its current form predicts nothing because it produces everything. The reply from Guth, Linde and Kaiser is that the same could have been said of quantum field theory before the Standard Model. Both sides are arguing about what a framework owes, not about data.'}
  ]
 },
 phil:{
  stance:'The most impressive fit in science and the least explanatory. Six numbers reproduce the microwave sky, the light elements and the galaxy distribution, and four of those numbers refer to things nobody has isolated. ΛCDM should be read as a superb phenomenological parameterisation of a cosmic history whose ingredients are named after their gravitational effects — genuinely predictive at the level of relations among observations, and silent about what the universe is made of.',
  disputes:[
   'Ellis versus the mainstream on whether the cosmological principle is an observational result or a Copernican posit made for tractability.',
   'Buchert versus Green and Wald on whether averaging inhomogeneities produces a backreaction large enough to mimic dark energy.',
   'Steinhardt, Ijjas and Loeb versus Guth, Linde, Kaiser and co-signatories (2017) on whether inflation is testable at all given eternal inflation and the measure problem.',
   'Whether the H₀ tension is a systematic in the Cepheid calibration (Freedman, Carnegie–Chicago) or evidence of new pre-recombination physics (Riess and SH0ES).',
   'Smolin and Unger on whether a theory of a single, unrepeatable object can support the modal claims cosmology routinely makes.'
  ],
  read:['Ellis, "Issues in the Philosophy of Cosmology" (2007)','Earman & Mosterín, "A Critical Look at Inflationary Cosmology" (1999)','Weinberg, *Cosmology*','Peebles, *Cosmology\'s Century*','Smeenk, "Philosophy of Cosmology" (2013)','Ijjas, Steinhardt & Loeb, "Inflationary Paradigm in Trouble After Planck2013" (2013)']
 },
 fig:'lcdmeras'
},

{
 id:'astro', n:'Stellar Structure & Nucleosynthesis', aka:'Eddington · Chandrasekhar · Bethe · B²FH',
 dom:'physics', tier:3, years:'1926 · 1938 · 1957 –', status:'confirmed',
 scale:{len:'10⁴ – 10¹² m', en:'eV – 10 MeV', lg:8},
 one:'Four coupled differential equations, a nuclear reaction network and a table of opacities, which together take a mass and a composition and return a star\'s luminosity, radius, spectrum, lifetime and manner of death — and, as a by-product, the abundance of every chemical element heavier than lithium.',
 onto:'A self-gravitating sphere of fully ionised plasma in which pressure balances gravity, energy is generated by nuclear fusion in a central core and transported outward by photon diffusion or convective overturn. The "star" of the theory is one-dimensional, non-rotating, non-magnetic and spherically symmetric. Real stars are none of these things, and the discrepancies are absorbed into fitted parameters rather than derived.',
 state:'Four functions of the mass coordinate m ∈ [0,M] — radius r(m), pressure P(m), luminosity L(m) and temperature T(m) — together with composition fractions X_i(m,t) evolved by a reaction network. The boundary conditions are r = L = 0 at the centre and a photospheric condition at the surface, which makes it a two-point boundary-value problem solved by relaxation (Henyey).',

 axioms:[
  {n:'There is no axiomatisation; these are reconstructed constitutive commitments',
   s:'Stellar structure theory is applied hydrodynamics, thermodynamics and nuclear physics, not an axiomatic system; what follows is what a practitioner would defend if pressed.',
   t:'Worth stating because the field\'s confidence is often mistaken for deductive security. Nothing here is derived from a shorter list; the "standard model of the Sun" is a computer program with about a dozen physical inputs, several of which are tabulated fits. Its authority comes from agreement with helioseismology and neutrinos, not from its foundations.'},
  {n:'Hydrostatic equilibrium',
   s:R`\frac{dP}{dm}=-\frac{Gm}{4\pi r^{4}}`,
   t:'The single most load-bearing assumption. It holds because the dynamical timescale of the Sun is about 27 minutes while its thermal timescale is 3×10⁷ years and its nuclear timescale 10¹⁰ years — a separation of 14 orders of magnitude. It fails outright during collapse, in pulsating variables (where it is replaced by a linearised wave equation) and in the outer layers of red supergiants.'},
  {n:'Spherical symmetry, with no rotation and no magnetic field',
   s:R`\partial_{\theta}=\partial_{\phi}=0;\qquad \Omega=0,\ \mathbf{B}=0`,
   t:'Reduces a partial differential system to an ordinary one and is the reason stellar models can be computed at all. The Sun\'s rotational distortion is about 10⁻⁵ in oblateness, so the idealisation is excellent there and poor for a rapid rotator like Achernar, which is flattened by roughly 30% and cannot be described by a one-dimensional model at any level.'},
  {n:'Local thermodynamic equilibrium',
   s:R`\text{At each point the matter has a local } T:\ \ B_{\nu}(T),\ \text{Saha and Boltzmann populations hold.}`,
   t:'Justified because the photon mean free path — about 1 cm in the solar interior — is minute compared with the scale height, so radiation is nearly isotropic and nearly Planckian. It breaks down in the photosphere and chromosphere, where spectral line formation is explicitly non-LTE, and this matters: the downward revision of the solar oxygen abundance that created the present solar modelling problem came from a non-LTE, three-dimensional reanalysis of exactly those layers.'},
  {n:'Energy transport is radiative diffusion, or convection where the gradient is superadiabatic',
   s:R`\nabla_{\rm rad}>\nabla_{\rm ad}\ \Rightarrow\ \text{convective (Schwarzschild criterion)}`,
   t:'The criterion itself is a clean local stability result. What it does not supply is the convective flux once instability sets in — that requires a theory of turbulence, which does not exist, and is supplied by the mixing-length prescription below.'},
  {n:'Mixing-length theory of convection, with a fitted parameter',
   s:R`\ell_{\rm mix}=\alpha_{\rm MLT}H_{P},\qquad \alpha_{\rm MLT}\approx1.5\text{–}2.2\ \text{(fitted)}`,
   t:'This is a fudge and should be labelled as one. Böhm-Vitense (1958) modelled convection as blobs travelling one mixing length before dissolving; α is set by requiring a solar model to reproduce the Sun\'s observed radius at its known age. It is then used unchanged for stars of other masses, metallicities and evolutionary states. Three-dimensional radiation-hydrodynamic simulations (Trampedach, Magic and others) show the effective α varies by tens of percent across the HR diagram, so the universality is known to be false and is retained for want of anything better.'},
  {n:'Local energy generation and a closed reaction network',
   s:R`\frac{dL}{dm}=\varepsilon_{\rm nuc}(\rho,T,X_{i})-\varepsilon_{\nu}+\varepsilon_{\rm grav}`,
   t:'Rates come from laboratory cross-sections extrapolated to stellar energies, typically tens of keV, far below where they can be measured directly — the Gamow window sits below the Coulomb barrier and the cross-sections there are of order 10⁻¹² barn. The extrapolation uses the astrophysical S-factor and is the dominant uncertainty in solar-neutrino predictions.'},
  {n:'The Vogt–Russell conjecture',
   s:'Mass and chemical composition determine a star\'s structure and evolution uniquely.',
   t:'Called a theorem in older textbooks; it is not one, and counterexamples exist — thermally unstable shell-burning stars can have multiple equilibrium configurations for the same mass and composition. It survives as a working rule because the exceptions occupy short-lived evolutionary phases.'}
 ],

 hyps:[
  {h:'Stars shine by thermonuclear fusion of hydrogen into helium.',
   k:'core',
   b:'Eddington argued for subatomic energy in 1920 from the timescale problem and was told the interior was too cool for protons to overcome the Coulomb barrier; Gamow\'s tunnelling (1928) removed the objection, and Bethe and von Weizsäcker supplied the specific cycles in 1938–39. Direct confirmation came only with neutrino detection. If false, stars would live for 3×10⁷ years and the Earth\'s geological record would be impossible.'},
  {h:'Elements heavier than helium were synthesised in stars, not in the big bang.',
   k:'core',
   b:'B²FH and Cameron, both 1957. It replaced Gamow\'s proposal that all elements formed in the first minutes, which fails at the mass-5 and mass-8 gaps — there are no stable nuclei with A = 5 or 8, so a hot big bang cannot build past lithium in the time available. The abundance pattern with its iron peak, s-process and r-process signatures is now traced to specific sites.'},
  {h:'Opacity tables computed from atomic physics give the true photon mean free path.',
   k:'bridge',
   b:'κ(ρ,T,composition) converts microscopic atomic structure into a macroscopic transport coefficient. It is the hinge on which the mass–luminosity relation swings. The OPAL revision of the early 1990s roughly doubled the iron-group opacity at a few hundred thousand kelvin, which resolved the long-standing Cepheid mass and beat-period discrepancies — a case of a stellar problem being solved by redoing atomic physics.'},
  {h:'The convective mixing length is a fixed multiple of the pressure scale height, the same in all stars.',
   k:'auxiliary',
   b:'Introduced for tractability, calibrated on the Sun, and known to be wrong in detail. If it goes, the radii and effective temperatures of every cool star model shift by amounts comparable to the precision of modern asteroseismology, and stellar ages — including globular cluster ages used as a cosmological cross-check — move by several percent. This is the clearest example in the atlas of a fitted parameter carrying a large downstream inferential load.'},
  {h:'Convective overshoot, rotational mixing and mass loss are each described by a one-parameter prescription.',
   k:'auxiliary',
   b:'Overshoot distance α_ov ≈ 0.2 H_P, Reimers or Blöcker mass-loss laws with efficiency η, diffusion coefficients for rotational instabilities — all fitted to observed populations rather than derived. Massive-star evolution is especially sensitive: the final mass, the supernova type and the black-hole mass function all depend on mass-loss prescriptions uncertain by factors of two.'},
  {h:'The Sun\'s interior contracts gravitationally, and that contraction is its energy source.',
   k:'abandoned',
   b:'Kelvin and Helmholtz, 1850s–60s. It gives a solar lifetime of about 3×10⁷ years, which Kelvin used against Darwin and Lyell. Replaced by nuclear fusion, which gives 10¹⁰ years. The episode is a well-documented case of a confident physical argument overruling well-founded geological inference and being wrong because of physics not yet discovered.'},
  {h:'Neutrinos are massless, so the flavour produced in the Sun is the flavour that arrives.',
   k:'abandoned',
   b:'Assumed throughout the construction of the standard solar model and used to interpret Homestake. Its falsity is what resolved the solar neutrino problem: the deficit was in the detector\'s flavour sensitivity, not in the star. Bahcall spent thirty years insisting the solar models were right and was vindicated by particle physics, not by astrophysics.'}
 ],

 preds:[
  {p:'A resonance must exist in ¹²C at about 7.7 MeV, or carbon cannot be made in stars.',
   num:R`E_{x}=7.65\ \mathrm{MeV},\ J^{\pi}=0^{+}`,
   st:'confirmed',
   b:'Hoyle argued in 1953 that the observed carbon abundance required a resonant enhancement of the triple-alpha rate, and persuaded Fowler\'s Kellogg group at Caltech to look; Dunbar, Pixley, Wenzel and Whaling found the state within months at 7.68 ± 0.03 MeV. A genuine prior prediction from an abundance argument. Kragh has shown the later gloss of it as an anthropic prediction is largely retrospective embroidery.'},
  {p:'The Sun emits about 6.5×10¹⁰ electron neutrinos per cm² per second, dominated by the pp reaction.',
   num:R`\Phi(^{8}\mathrm{B})=5.05^{+0.20}_{-0.16}\times10^{6}\ \mathrm{cm^{-2}s^{-1}}\ (\mathrm{SSM})`,
   st:'failed',
   b:'Davis\'s Homestake experiment measured 2.56 ± 0.23 SNU against a predicted 8.5 ± 1.8 SNU — about one third, from 1968 onward. For thirty years this was read by most physicists as a failure of the solar model. SNO settled it in 2001–02 by measuring the neutral-current rate, which is flavour-blind: the total flux was 5.09 ×10⁶ cm⁻²s⁻¹, matching the model, and the missing electron neutrinos had oscillated. The prediction failed and the theory that failed was particle physics.'},
  {p:'The CNO cycle contributes about one percent of solar luminosity and produces a detectable neutrino flux.',
   num:R`\Phi(\mathrm{CNO})=7.0^{+3.0}_{-2.0}\times10^{8}\ \mathrm{cm^{-2}s^{-1}}`,
   st:'confirmed',
   b:'Bethe\'s 1939 cycle, detected in 2020 by Borexino at 5σ after reducing the bismuth-210 background in a 280-tonne liquid scintillator held thermally stable to millikelvin. Eighty-one years between prediction and direct observation. The measured rate is consistent with both high- and low-metallicity solar compositions, so it did not yet settle the abundance problem it was hoped to settle.'},
  {p:'A cold degenerate star cannot exceed about 1.4 solar masses.',
   num:R`M_{\rm Ch}=1.456\,(2/\mu_{e})^{2}\,M_{\odot}\approx1.44\,M_{\odot}`,
   st:'confirmed',
   b:'Chandrasekhar computed it in 1930, aged 19, on the voyage from Madras to Cambridge; Eddington ridiculed it publicly in 1935 and the field largely deferred to Eddington for a decade. Observed white dwarf masses cluster near 0.6 M⊙ and none exceeds the limit; the limit also sets the Type Ia supernova scale. The derivation is unusually clean — Fermi–Dirac statistics plus a relativistic dispersion relation, with no fitted parameters.'},
  {p:'The five-minute solar oscillation is a superposition of trapped global acoustic modes.',
   num:R`\nu\approx3\ \mathrm{mHz},\ \text{ridges in the}\ (\ell,\nu)\ \text{diagram}`,
   st:'confirmed',
   b:'Leighton found the oscillation in 1960 and it was taken for local convective overshoot. Ulrich (1970) and Leibacher and Stein (1971) predicted that if the modes were global standing waves they would form discrete ridges in a wavenumber–frequency diagram. Deubner observed the ridges in 1975. This is the prediction that created helioseismology.'},
  {p:'The interior sound speed profile computed from the standard solar model matches the measured one.',
   num:R`\delta c/c\lesssim 0.4\%\ \text{rms; convection-zone base at } 0.713\pm0.001\,R_{\odot}`,
   st:'confirmed',
   b:'Inversion of about 10⁵ measured p-mode frequencies from BiSON, GONG and SOHO/MDI gives the sound speed from 0.05 to 0.95 R⊙. The agreement with models built before the data existed is at the few-parts-per-thousand level — better than the agreement of most laboratory equations of state with experiment.'},
  {p:'The same agreement survives the revised, lower solar metallicity.',
   num:R`\text{Z/X}:\ 0.0245\to0.0181;\ \delta c/c\ \text{worsens to}\ \sim1\%\ \text{near the CZ base}`,
   st:'failed',
   b:'Asplund, Grevesse and Sauval\'s three-dimensional non-LTE analysis (2005, 2009) lowered the solar oxygen and carbon abundances by about 30%. The new composition is better spectroscopy and produces a worse solar model: the convection-zone depth, helium abundance and sound speed all move outside the helioseismic error bars. Twenty years on, the "solar abundance problem" is unresolved; proposed fixes invoke opacity increases, accretion of metal-poor material, or dark-matter energy transport, none established.'},
  {p:'The rapid neutron-capture process occurs in neutron star mergers.',
   num:R`\sim0.05\,M_{\odot}\ \text{of r-process ejecta from GW170817}`,
   st:'confirmed',
   b:'B²FH described the r-process in 1957 without identifying a site; core-collapse supernovae were the favoured candidate for fifty years and modern simulations fail to produce the required entropy. The kilonova AT2017gfo following GW170817 in August 2017 showed a reddening, lanthanide-opacity light curve consistent with several hundredths of a solar mass of heavy-element ejecta. A nuclear-astrophysics question settled by a gravitational-wave detection.'}
 ],

 eqs:[
  {tex:R`\frac{dr}{dm}=\frac{1}{4\pi r^{2}\rho},\quad \frac{dP}{dm}=-\frac{Gm}{4\pi r^{4}},\quad \frac{dL}{dm}=\varepsilon,\quad \frac{dT}{dm}=-\frac{3\kappa L}{256\pi^{2}\sigma r^{4}T^{3}}`, name:'The four equations of stellar structure',
   gloss:'Mass conservation, hydrostatic balance, energy conservation and radiative transport, written in the Lagrangian mass coordinate because m is conserved as the star expands and contracts. The fourth equation is replaced by the adiabatic gradient in convective regions. Everything a star does on the main sequence is in these four lines plus three material functions ρ(P,T), κ(ρ,T) and ε(ρ,T).'},
  {tex:R`L\propto \frac{M^{3}\mu^{4}}{\kappa}\ \ \Rightarrow\ \ L\propto M^{3.5}\ \ (M\gtrsim M_{\odot}),\qquad t_{\rm MS}\approx10\ \mathrm{Gyr}\left(\frac{M}{M_{\odot}}\right)^{-2.5}`, name:'Mass–luminosity relation',
   gloss:'Obtained by dimensional analysis on the structure equations with no nuclear physics whatsoever: luminosity is fixed by how fast radiation leaks out, not by how fast fuel burns. Eddington got this in 1924 before anyone knew what powered stars, which is why the relation is a striking case of structure constraining output independently of mechanism. The nuclear rate then adjusts to supply exactly that luminosity.'},
  {tex:R`\varepsilon_{pp}\propto \rho X^{2}T^{4},\qquad \varepsilon_{\rm CNO}\propto \rho X X_{\rm CNO}T^{\sim18},\qquad \varepsilon_{3\alpha}\propto \rho^{2}Y^{3}T^{\sim40}`, name:'Nuclear energy generation rates',
   gloss:'The exponents are local logarithmic derivatives, not true power laws — they vary with temperature. The extreme steepness follows from tunnelling through the Coulomb barrier, which makes the rate exponentially sensitive to T. It is why the CNO cycle overtakes the pp chain above about 1.3 M⊙ and 17 MK, why massive stars have convective cores, and why helium burning is thermally explosive in degenerate matter.'},
  {tex:R`P_{\rm deg}=K_{1}\left(\frac{\rho}{\mu_{e}}\right)^{5/3}\ \to\ K_{2}\left(\frac{\rho}{\mu_{e}}\right)^{4/3},\qquad M_{\rm Ch}\approx\frac{(\hbar c/G)^{3/2}}{(\mu_{e}m_{u})^{2}}\approx1.44\,M_{\odot}`, name:'Degeneracy pressure and the Chandrasekhar mass',
   gloss:'The 5/3 index supports a white dwarf; the relativistic 4/3 index does not, because a polytrope of index 3 has a mass independent of radius. The limit is built from ħ, c, G and the proton mass alone — one of the very few macroscopic quantities in astrophysics assembled purely out of fundamental constants, with no fitted number anywhere in it.'},
  {tex:R`L_{\rm Edd}=\frac{4\pi G M m_{p}c}{\sigma_{T}}\approx1.26\times10^{31}\left(\frac{M}{M_{\odot}}\right)\ \mathrm{W}`, name:'Eddington luminosity',
   gloss:'The luminosity at which radiation pressure on free electrons balances gravity on the ions to which they are electrostatically bound. It caps stellar masses near 150–300 M⊙ and sets the accretion rate of black holes. Apparently super-Eddington sources exist and are explained by anisotropy or clumping, so the limit is a spherically symmetric bound, not a law.'},
  {tex:R`\sigma(E)=\frac{S(E)}{E}\exp\!\left(-2\pi\eta\right),\qquad 2\pi\eta=\frac{2\pi Z_{1}Z_{2}e^{2}}{4\pi\varepsilon_{0}\hbar v}`, name:'Astrophysical S-factor and the Gamow peak',
   gloss:'The Coulomb penetrability is factored out so the residual S(E) varies slowly and can be extrapolated from laboratory energies of ~100 keV–1 MeV down to the ~5–30 keV Gamow window where measurement is impossible. The extrapolation is the single largest uncertainty in predicted neutrino fluxes, which is why underground accelerators such as LUNA exist.'},
  {tex:R`\nu_{n\ell m}\approx \Delta\nu\left(n+\frac{\ell}{2}+\epsilon\right)-\delta\nu,\qquad \Delta\nu=\left[2\int_{0}^{R}\frac{dr}{c(r)}\right]^{-1}`, name:'Asymptotic p-mode frequencies',
   gloss:'The large frequency separation is the inverse sound travel time across the star, so it measures mean density directly; the small separation probes the core and hence the age. Helioseismology inverts about 10⁵ such frequencies for c(r) and Ω(r). Asteroseismology now does the same for tens of thousands of stars observed by Kepler and TESS.'}
 ],
 concepts:[
  {t:'The HR diagram is not a plot of observables', d:'Hertzsprung (1911) and Russell (1913) plotted absolute magnitude against spectral type, which are observables. The theoretical version plots log L against log T_eff, and converting between them requires bolometric corrections, a temperature scale and an extinction estimate — every one of which is model-dependent. The main sequence is a mass sequence, not an evolutionary track: stars move along it hardly at all, which was the central misreading of the diagram for two decades.'},
  {t:'Thermal stability and the negative heat capacity of self-gravitating systems', d:'A star that loses energy gets hotter. From the virial theorem 2K + U = 0, so E = −K: removing energy raises the internal temperature. This is why main-sequence burning is stable — an excess in the nuclear rate expands and cools the core — and why degenerate ignition is not, since degenerate pressure does not respond to temperature. The helium flash and Type Ia supernovae both follow from that single failure of the thermostat.'},
  {t:'Convection and the mixing-length parameter', d:'Instability sets in when the radiative gradient exceeds the adiabatic one. What happens next is turbulent convection at Rayleigh numbers of order 10²⁰, utterly beyond simulation in a full star. Mixing-length theory replaces it with blobs that travel αH_P and dissolve. α is calibrated on the Sun and then exported. Every published stellar age, including the globular-cluster ages used to cross-check cosmology, depends on that exported number.'},
  {t:'Nuclear statistical equilibrium and the iron peak', d:'Above about 4 GK, forward and reverse nuclear reactions balance and abundances depend only on ρ, T and the electron fraction, not on rates. The equilibrium distribution peaks at the most tightly bound nuclei near A = 56. This is why the iron peak in the cosmic abundance curve exists, and why it is a thermodynamic rather than a kinetic feature — a rare case in astrophysics where an abundance pattern needs no reaction network at all.'},
  {t:'The s- and r-processes', d:'Slow neutron capture, with τ_capture ≫ τ_β, walks up the valley of stability in AGB star shells; rapid capture, with τ_capture ≪ τ_β, drives matter to the neutron drip line and produces the abundance peaks at A = 80, 130 and 195, offset from the s-process peaks because they form at magic neutron numbers before decaying back. The offset of the two peak sets was the original evidence, in 1957, that two distinct processes must exist.'},
  {t:'Endpoints', d:'Below ~8 M⊙ a star ends as a carbon–oxygen white dwarf; between ~8 and ~20 M⊙ as a neutron star via core collapse; above that, in a regime where the outcome depends on metallicity and mass loss, as a black hole, often with no visible supernova. The mapping from initial mass to remnant is not a clean function and is currently constrained more tightly by the LIGO black-hole mass distribution than by stellar theory.'},
  {t:'The solar modelling problem', d:'The Sun is the one star whose interior is measured, and the standard model no longer fits it once the best available surface abundances are used. Either the spectroscopy is wrong, or the opacities are, or the Sun accreted metal-poor material, or something is missing. It is a live anomaly in the best-tested stellar model there is, and it deserves more attention from philosophers of science than it gets.'}
 ],
 results:[
  {n:'Eddington\'s mass–luminosity relation (1924)', s:'L ∝ M^3.5 over three decades of mass, derived without knowing the energy source and confirmed by eclipsing binaries to about 10%.'},
  {n:'The Chandrasekhar limit', s:'1.44 M⊙ for μ_e = 2, built from ħ, c, G and m_u alone. No observed white dwarf exceeds it; the highest reliable masses are near 1.35 M⊙.'},
  {n:'B²FH (1957)', s:'A 108-page paper assigning every element to a process — H burning, He burning, α, e, s, r, p, x — and predicting the abundance curve from nuclear systematics. Fowler received the Nobel in 1983; Hoyle did not.'},
  {n:'Resolution of the solar neutrino problem (2001–02)', s:'SNO neutral-current flux 5.09 (+0.44/−0.43) ×10⁶ cm⁻²s⁻¹ against a standard-solar-model prediction of 5.05 ×10⁶. The astrophysics was right to within 1%; the particle physics was wrong.'},
  {n:'Helioseismic sound speed', s:'Inversions agree with pre-existing models to better than 0.4% rms from 0.05 to 0.95 R⊙, and locate the base of the convection zone at 0.713 ± 0.001 R⊙.'},
  {n:'Asteroseismic ages and radii', s:'Kepler and TESS deliver stellar radii to ~2% and ages to ~10% for tens of thousands of stars, which is what makes exoplanet radii and Galactic archaeology quantitative.'},
  {n:'SN 1987A neutrinos', s:'Kamiokande-II, IMB and Baksan detected 24 neutrino events in about 13 seconds on 23 February 1987 — confirming that core collapse releases ~3×10⁴⁶ J, 99% of it as neutrinos, as predicted.'}
 ],
 valid:{
  holds:'Single, slowly rotating, non-magnetic stars from about 0.1 to 100 M⊙ on the main sequence and through most post-main-sequence evolution. Solar models reproduce the interior sound speed to a few parts per thousand and neutrino fluxes to their extrapolation uncertainty.',
  breaks:'Rapid rotators (oblateness up to 30%), close binaries with mass transfer (roughly half of massive stars), magnetically active low-mass stars whose radii exceed models by 5–10%, the convective surface layers where one-dimensional models fail outright, and every dynamical phase — collapse, thermonuclear runaway, common-envelope evolution — where hydrostatic equilibrium is abandoned.'
 },
 detail:{
  deriv:[
   {h:'Why luminosity is set by opacity and not by nuclear physics', b:'Take the radiative transport equation and the hydrostatic equation, and eliminate the radius by dimensional substitution: P ~ GM²/R⁴ · R, T ~ μGM/(k_B R) from the ideal gas law and virial scaling, and L ~ (R T⁴)/(κρ) · R² from diffusion. Combining gives L ∝ G⁴μ⁴M³/κ, with no ε anywhere in it. The nuclear rate has dropped out. The physical statement is that a star\'s luminosity is fixed by how fast energy can escape, and the core temperature then adjusts until the exponentially sensitive nuclear rate supplies exactly that much. Turn the nuclear rate up by a factor of ten and the core cools by a few percent and the luminosity does not change. This is why Eddington could obtain the relation in 1924 without knowing about fusion, and it is a worked instance of a general structural point: in a system with a strong thermostat, the output is set by the resistance, not by the source. It is also why the opacity table is the most consequential input to a stellar model, and why a purely atomic-physics revision — OPAL in 1992 — resolved a decades-old discrepancy in Cepheid pulsation that everyone had been attributing to stellar dynamics.'},
   {h:'The Chandrasekhar derivation, and where the assumption enters', b:'Electrons in a cold dense gas fill momentum states up to p_F with n_e = 8π p_F³/3h³. Non-relativistically the pressure integral gives P ∝ n^{5/3}; when p_F c ≫ m_ec², the dispersion relation changes to E ≈ pc and the same integral gives P ∝ n^{4/3}. Insert either into hydrostatic equilibrium and the star is a Lane–Emden polytrope. For P ∝ ρ^{1+1/n} with n = 3 — the relativistic case — the mass of the solution is independent of the central density, so there is exactly one mass at which a relativistic degenerate configuration can exist: M_Ch = 5.836/μ_e² M⊙, which is 1.456 M⊙ for μ_e = 2. The assumption that enters is that the electrons are the only pressure source and the ions contribute inertia only, together with T = 0 exactly. Both are excellent for a white dwarf. The result was rejected by Eddington in 1935 on the grounds that nature would "find a way" to avoid the conclusion; his objection was to the relativistic degeneracy formula itself, and it was simply wrong. Chandrasekhar left stellar structure for a decade afterwards. The Nobel came in 1983, fifty-three years after the calculation.'},
   {h:'What the solar neutrino problem was actually a test of', b:'Homestake measured a rate, in solar neutrino units, of chlorine-37 converting to argon-37. To turn that into a statement about the Sun requires: the ³⁷Cl cross-section including excited-state contributions (Bahcall\'s work, and a real source of uncertainty), the extraction efficiency for a few atoms of argon in 615 tonnes of perchloroethylene, the cosmic-ray background, and the predicted ⁸B flux, which depends on the ⁷Be(p,γ)⁸B cross-section extrapolated from laboratory energies. Any of these could have been wrong, and the deficit was a factor of three. The community\'s response was Duhemian in the textbook sense: the ⁸B flux scales roughly as T_c^{18}, so a 5% error in central temperature would explain everything, and many physicists concluded the solar model was at fault. Bahcall resisted for thirty years on the grounds that helioseismology constrained T_c far more tightly than that. The resolution required an experiment — SNO — that measured the same neutrinos through a flavour-blind channel, so that the ratio of charged-current to neutral-current rates isolated oscillation from flux. The decisive move was not better theory but an observable that separated the two hypotheses.'}
  ],
  deeper:[
   {h:'A fitted parameter carrying a cosmological inference', b:'Consider the chain by which globular cluster ages constrain cosmology. Observed colour–magnitude diagrams are compared with isochrones; the main-sequence turn-off luminosity gives the age. The isochrone depends on the mixing-length parameter through the radius of the model and hence its effective temperature; on convective overshoot through the core mass; on the helium abundance; on the opacity tables; and on the distance scale. In the mid-1990s this chain gave cluster ages of 16–18 Gyr, older than the then-favoured Einstein–de Sitter universe, and the conflict was a standard argument for a low-density or Λ-dominated cosmology. Hipparcos parallaxes then revised the distance scale and the ages fell to about 13 Gyr. The philosophical point is not that the earlier work was careless — it was careful — but that a cosmological conclusion was resting on a convection parameter calibrated against a single star, and nobody in the cosmology literature was tracking that dependency. Stellar astrophysics is full of such quiet load-bearing fits, and its practitioners are generally candid about them in a way that consumers of their results are not.'},
   {h:'Model-dependence all the way down', b:'Stellar structure is often presented as physics\' success story about reduction: microscopic nuclear cross-sections and atomic opacities feed macroscopic structure equations and out comes a star. Look at what actually enters a modern code — MESA, say — and the picture is more mixed. Opacities are tabulated from atomic-structure calculations that cannot be checked at stellar conditions, though the Sandia Z-machine measurement of iron opacity in 2015 found values 30–400% above the models and has not been explained. Equations of state are tabulated. Nuclear rates are extrapolated below the measurable range. Convection, overshoot, rotational mixing, magnetic braking and mass loss are each parameterised. The reaction network is truncated. And yet the models reproduce the Sun\'s interior sound speed to a few parts per thousand. This combination — deep dependence on fits, together with high accuracy against independent data — is exactly what Cartwright\'s dappled picture predicts and what a straightforward reductionist account does not. The right description is probably neither: it is a tightly constrained interpolation between many independent measurements, whose accuracy comes from the number of constraints rather than from the derivational purity of any one of them.'}
  ]
 },
 phil:{
  stance:'The best available demonstration that accuracy and derivational purity come apart. A solar model is a computer program with a dozen tabulated or fitted inputs, at least one of which — the mixing length — is an admitted fudge; it nevertheless predicts an unmeasurable interior to within a few parts per thousand, as verified by two entirely independent probes, seismic and neutrino. Whatever makes that work, it is not derivation from below.',
  disputes:[
   'Whether the solar abundance problem indicates an error in three-dimensional non-LTE spectroscopy (Asplund and collaborators) or missing opacity, with the 2015 Sandia iron measurement unexplained on either view.',
   'Whether mixing-length theory should be retained with a calibrated parameter or replaced by three-dimensional simulation-derived prescriptions that vary across the HR diagram.',
   'Whether the anthropic gloss on Hoyle\'s carbon resonance is a genuine case of anthropic prediction (Barrow and Tipler, Carr and Rees) or a retrospective construction (Kragh, 2010).',
   'Whether core-collapse supernovae contribute significantly to the r-process at all, now that neutron star mergers are known to.'
  ],
  read:['Kippenhahn, Weigert & Weiss, *Stellar Structure and Evolution*','Burbidge, Burbidge, Fowler & Hoyle, "Synthesis of the Elements in Stars" (1957)','Bahcall, *Neutrino Astrophysics*','Chandrasekhar, *An Introduction to the Study of Stellar Structure*','Kragh, "An Anthropic Myth: Fred Hoyle\'s Carbon-12 Resonance Level" (2010)','Christensen-Dalsgaard, "Helioseismology" (2002)']
 },
 fig:'hrdiag'
},

{
 id:'plasma', n:'Plasma Physics & Magnetohydrodynamics', aka:'Langmuir · Vlasov · Landau · Alfvén',
 dom:'physics', tier:4, years:'1928 · 1938 · 1946 · 1942 –', status:'effective',
 scale:{len:'10⁻⁵ – 10²² m', en:'eV – 10 keV', lg:27},
 one:'The physics of matter in which charges are free and interactions are collective rather than binary, described by a hierarchy of closures running from an exact microscopic distribution down to a single magnetised fluid — each rung obtained by an ordering assumption, and the lowest rung used far outside the regime where its derivation is valid.',
 onto:'Charged particles and the electromagnetic field they collectively generate, with no clean separation between the two: the field is a degree of freedom determined by the particles and simultaneously determining them. At the kinetic level the object is a distribution function on six-dimensional phase space; at the fluid level, density, velocity and a magnetic field threaded through the material. The magnetic field in ideal MHD acquires an odd quasi-material status, being carried by the fluid and exerting tension on it.',
 state:'Kinetic: f_s(x,v,t) for each species, one function on six-dimensional phase space, coupled to E and B. Fluid: (ρ, u, p, B) on three-dimensional space, with ∇·B = 0 as a constraint propagated by the evolution rather than imposed at each step. The reduction from the first to the second discards all information about the velocity distribution beyond its first three moments.',

 axioms:[
  {n:'No independent axioms: Maxwell plus Lorentz, plus a hierarchy of closures',
   s:R`m_{s}\dot{\mathbf{v}}_{i}=q_{s}(\mathbf{E}+\mathbf{v}_{i}\times\mathbf{B}),\quad \text{with } \mathbf{E},\mathbf{B}\ \text{sourced by the charges themselves.}`,
   t:'Plasma physics adds no fundamental postulate to electrodynamics and mechanics. What it adds is a sequence of approximations — Klimontovich to Vlasov to two-fluid to MHD — each licensed by an ordering. The interesting foundational content is entirely in those orderings, which is unusual: the field\'s axioms are other fields\' theorems.'},
  {n:'Debye shielding and quasineutrality',
   s:R`\lambda_{D}=\sqrt{\frac{\varepsilon_{0}k_{B}T_{e}}{n_{e}e^{2}}},\qquad L\gg\lambda_{D}\ \Rightarrow\ n_{e}\approx\sum_{i}Z_{i}n_{i}`,
   t:'A test charge is screened over λ_D, so the long-range Coulomb interaction becomes effectively short-ranged and the many-body problem becomes tractable. Quasineutrality is a consequence, not a separate assumption: charge separation over L ≫ λ_D would require energies far above the thermal energy. It fails in sheaths at material boundaries, which is where all laboratory plasmas meet their walls.'},
  {n:'The plasma parameter is large',
   s:R`\Lambda = n\lambda_{D}^{3}\gg1`,
   t:'Many particles inside a Debye sphere, so the mean potential energy between neighbours is small compared with the kinetic energy and collective fields dominate binary collisions. Λ ≈ 10⁸ in a tokamak, ~10⁹ in the solar wind, but of order unity in white-dwarf interiors and inertial-confinement fuel, where the whole framework fails and one needs strongly coupled plasma theory instead.'},
  {n:'Vlasov closure: the mean field replaces the exact microscopic field',
   s:R`\frac{\partial f_{s}}{\partial t}+\mathbf{v}\!\cdot\!\nabla f_{s}+\frac{q_{s}}{m_{s}}(\mathbf{E}+\mathbf{v}\times\mathbf{B})\!\cdot\!\nabla_{v}f_{s}=0`,
   t:'Obtained by ensemble-averaging the exact Klimontovich equation and dropping the two-particle correlation term, which is O(1/Λ). The result is time-reversible, entropy-conserving and Hamiltonian. The discarded term reappears one order down as the Landau–Fokker–Planck collision operator, and that is where irreversibility actually enters — not, as is sometimes said, in Landau damping.'},
  {n:'The MHD ordering',
   s:R`\omega\ll\Omega_{ci},\quad L\gg\rho_{i},\quad u\ll c,\quad \beta\sim1,\quad \text{and (for the single-fluid closure) } \lambda_{\rm mfp}\ll L`,
   t:'Five conditions that together collapse two kinetic species to one conducting fluid. Slow compared with the ion gyrofrequency, large compared with the ion gyroradius, non-relativistic so the displacement current drops, and collisional enough for the pressure to be a scalar. The last is violated in every plasma to which MHD is most successfully applied — a point taken up below.'},
  {n:'Ideal Ohm\'s law',
   s:R`\mathbf{E}+\mathbf{u}\times\mathbf{B}=0`,
   t:'The defining postulate of ideal MHD and the source of flux freezing. It is the η → 0 limit of the resistive law, and the limit is singular: no matter how small η is, there are always regions — current sheets — where the neglected term dominates. Dropping it is what allows reconnection, and reconnection is what makes solar flares, substorms and sawtooth crashes possible.'},
  {n:'The magnetic field is divergence-free and its evolution preserves topology in the ideal limit',
   s:R`\nabla\!\cdot\!\mathbf{B}=0;\qquad \frac{\partial\mathbf{B}}{\partial t}=\nabla\times(\mathbf{u}\times\mathbf{B})\ \Rightarrow\ \frac{d}{dt}\int_{S(t)}\mathbf{B}\!\cdot\!d\mathbf{S}=0`,
   t:'Alfvén\'s frozen-flux theorem, 1942. Field lines become material curves, and magnetic helicity ∫A·B dV becomes a topological invariant. Alfvén himself later campaigned against over-use of the frozen-in picture, arguing in 1976 that it was being applied in exactly the situations — with parallel electric fields and double layers — where it fails.'}
 ],

 hyps:[
  {h:'Collective, Debye-screened interactions dominate over binary Coulomb collisions.',
   k:'core',
   b:'Langmuir\'s insight in the 1920s and the reason the subject exists as something other than a gas of charges. It is what makes the Vlasov description sensible and what produces plasma oscillations at ω_pe, Debye sheaths and collective instabilities. If Λ is not large the plasma is strongly coupled and none of the standard apparatus applies: this is why warm dense matter is a separate field with separate methods.'},
  {h:'Ideal MHD correctly describes the large-scale dynamics of hot, effectively collisionless plasmas.',
   k:'auxiliary',
   b:'This is the field\'s most useful and least justified assumption. In the solar corona the ion mean free path is of order 10⁷ m and structures are 10⁷ m; in a tokamak the mean free path is kilometres and the machine is metres. The collisional closure that derives MHD is therefore violated by many orders of magnitude, and MHD stability predictions nevertheless work — the ideal ballooning and kink limits are respected by real discharges. The usual defence is that magnetisation across the field substitutes for collisions, and that the ideal-MHD energy principle is variational and so error-tolerant. Neither is a derivation.'},
  {h:'Cross-field transport in confinement devices is set by microturbulence, not by collisions.',
   k:'core',
   b:'Classical collisional transport underpredicts observed heat fluxes in tokamaks by one to two orders of magnitude; neoclassical theory, which adds the geometry of trapped-particle orbits, still underpredicts by roughly a factor of ten in the electron channel. The gap is "anomalous" transport, now attributed to ion-temperature-gradient and trapped-electron modes and computed with gyrokinetic codes such as GENE and GYRO. This attribution is well supported and expensive to check.'},
  {h:'Cross-field diffusion scales as Bohm diffusion, D ≈ k_BT/16eB.',
   k:'abandoned',
   b:'An empirical scaling from 1946 arc experiments which, extrapolated, made magnetic fusion look hopeless — the required device would have been implausibly large. It was displaced by gyro-Bohm scaling, D ∝ ρ_i²v_th/L, which is favourable and is what the current reactor programme relies on. A case where an empirical scaling law nearly closed a field and turned out to describe a regime rather than a mechanism.'},
  {h:'Magnetic reconnection proceeds at the Sweet–Parker rate.',
   k:'abandoned',
   b:'Sweet (1958) and Parker (1957) give a rate scaling as S^{-1/2} with the Lundquist number. For the solar corona S ≈ 10¹²–10¹⁴, so a flare would take months; flares take minutes. The resolution has several parts: Petschek\'s 1964 slow-shock geometry (which does not survive uniform-resistivity simulation), the plasmoid instability above S ≈ 10⁴, and collisionless Hall physics, which gives a rate of about 0.1 v_A independently of S. The Magnetospheric Multiscale mission measured the electron diffusion region directly in 2016.'},
  {h:'Confinement time in a tokamak follows the empirical IPB98(y,2) multi-machine scaling.',
   k:'auxiliary',
   b:'A log-linear regression over a database of several thousand discharges from about a dozen machines, giving τ_E ∝ I^{0.93}B^{0.15}P^{-0.69}n^{0.41}R^{1.97}. It is a fit, not a theory, and ITER\'s design point rests on extrapolating it by roughly a factor of two in linear size. Stating this plainly matters: a multi-billion-euro device is engineered around a regression whose underlying transport physics is not derived.'},
  {h:'Turbulence and transport can be computed from the gyrokinetic equation.',
   k:'heuristic',
   b:'Gyrokinetics averages over the fast gyration, reducing six dimensions to five and removing the gyrofrequency timescale. It guides the field\'s computational programme and reproduces measured fluctuation spectra and stiff temperature profiles. It is not itself under test in the way an ordinary hypothesis is: it is the framework within which transport hypotheses are formulated.'}
 ],

 preds:[
  {p:'A collisionless plasma wave damps even though the governing equation is time-reversible and collisionless.',
   num:R`\gamma=-\sqrt{\frac{\pi}{8}}\,\frac{\omega_{pe}}{(k\lambda_{D})^{3}}\exp\!\left[-\frac{1}{2k^{2}\lambda_{D}^{2}}-\frac{3}{2}\right]`,
   st:'confirmed',
   b:'Landau derived it in 1946 by treating the initial-value problem with a Laplace transform and deforming the contour around the pole at v = ω/k, against Vlasov\'s earlier normal-mode treatment which found no damping. It was widely disbelieved for eighteen years. Malmberg and Wharton measured it in 1964 in a cylindrical plasma column, exciting waves with one probe and detecting them with another, and found the exponential decay with the predicted dependence on kλ_D.'},
  {p:'The information apparently lost to Landau damping can be recovered: two damped waves produce a spontaneous third at a later time.',
   num:R`\tau_{\rm echo}=\frac{k_{2}}{k_{2}-k_{1}}\tau`,
   st:'confirmed',
   b:'Gould, O\'Neil and Malmberg predicted the plasma echo in 1967 and observed it shortly after. Excite a wave at k₁, let it damp away, excite another at k₂; at a calculable later time an echo appears at k₂ − k₁. The damping was phase mixing in velocity space, not dissipation — the information was in the fine structure of f(v) all along. This is the decisive experiment for the interpretive question below.'},
  {p:'A magnetised conducting fluid supports a transverse wave travelling along the field at B/√(μ₀ρ).',
   num:R`v_{A}=B/\sqrt{\mu_{0}\rho}`,
   st:'confirmed',
   b:'Alfvén predicted it in 1942 and was met with scepticism — the story that Fermi\'s "of course" at a 1948 Chicago seminar changed the field\'s mind is well attested. Lundquist demonstrated the waves in liquid mercury in 1949; Bostick and Levine in an ionised gas in 1952. They are now measured routinely in the solar wind and were identified in situ by Cluster and MMS.'},
  {p:'A differentially rotating, weakly magnetised disc is linearly unstable, supplying the turbulent viscosity accretion requires.',
   num:R`\frac{d\Omega^{2}}{d\ln R}<0\ \Rightarrow\ \text{instability};\ \ \alpha_{\rm SS}\sim10^{-2}`,
   st:'retrodiction',
   b:'Balbus and Hawley identified the magnetorotational instability in 1991 as the mechanism behind the α-viscosity that Shakura and Sunyaev had postulated in 1973 to fit observed accretion rates. The phenomenon — that discs accrete far faster than molecular viscosity allows — was known for two decades first. Velikhov and Chandrasekhar had derived the instability in the 1950s in a different context without noticing the application. Laboratory confirmation in liquid-metal Couette experiments remains partial.'},
  {p:'Reconnection in the magnetosphere occurs in an electron diffusion region with a characteristic crescent-shaped electron distribution.',
   num:R`\text{rate}\approx0.1\,v_{A};\ \ \mathbf{J}\!\cdot\!\mathbf{E}'>0\ \text{measured}`,
   st:'confirmed',
   b:'MMS flew four spacecraft in a tetrahedron with separations down to 10 km and 30 ms electron distribution cadence, and on 16 October 2015 crossed the magnetopause diffusion region, measuring the predicted crescent distributions and positive J·E′ where energy is converted. A prediction from kinetic simulation confirmed by a purpose-built mission.'},
  {p:'An axisymmetric magnetic field cannot be maintained by dynamo action.',
   num:R`—`,
   st:'confirmed',
   b:'Cowling\'s anti-dynamo theorem, 1933, a genuine no-go result: a steady axisymmetric field cannot be sustained by any axisymmetric flow. It is what forces all working dynamo models — solar, geodynamo, galactic — to break symmetry, and it killed a generation of simple models. Note what a theorem of this kind does: it does not predict a phenomenon, it forecloses a class of explanations, which is a different and underexamined form of scientific content.'},
  {p:'A deuterium–tritium tokamak of ITER\'s size reaches Q = 10 at 500 MW fusion power.',
   num:R`nT\tau_{E}\gtrsim3\times10^{21}\ \mathrm{keV\,s\,m^{-3}}`,
   st:'open',
   b:'The extrapolation rests on the IPB98(y,2) regression across roughly a factor of two in machine size and on the assumption that the H-mode pedestal, ELM control and disruption avoidance all scale as expected. JET reached Q ≈ 0.67 in 1997 and 69 MJ of fusion energy in a five-second pulse in 2023; the National Ignition Facility exceeded target gain unity in December 2022 with 3.15 MJ out for 2.05 MJ of laser energy in. Neither is the tokamak reactor regime.'}
 ],

 eqs:[
  {tex:R`\omega_{pe}=\sqrt{\frac{n_{e}e^{2}}{\varepsilon_{0}m_{e}}},\qquad f_{pe}\approx8980\sqrt{n_{e}[\mathrm{cm^{-3}}]}\ \mathrm{Hz}`, name:'Plasma frequency',
   gloss:'The natural oscillation of the electron fluid against the ions, and a hard cutoff: electromagnetic waves below ω_pe do not propagate. This is why the ionosphere reflects short-wave radio, why a spacecraft blacks out on re-entry, and why interferometry at a chosen frequency measures line-integrated density. It depends only on n_e, not on temperature — the one plasma parameter with no thermal content.'},
  {tex:R`\frac{\partial f}{\partial t}+\mathbf{v}\!\cdot\!\nabla f+\frac{q}{m}(\mathbf{E}+\mathbf{v}\times\mathbf{B})\!\cdot\!\frac{\partial f}{\partial \mathbf{v}}=C[f]`, name:'Kinetic equation, with and without collisions',
   gloss:'Set C = 0 and this is Vlasov: reversible, entropy-conserving, and the foundation of collisionless plasma theory. Keep C as the Landau operator and it is the plasma analogue of Boltzmann, with irreversibility. The subtlety the textbooks flatten is that Landau damping happens in the C = 0 version — so it cannot be dissipation in the thermodynamic sense.'},
  {tex:R`\varepsilon(k,\omega)=1+\sum_{s}\frac{\omega_{ps}^{2}}{k^{2}}\int \frac{\partial f_{s0}/\partial v}{v-\omega/k}\,dv=0`, name:'Plasma dispersion relation',
   gloss:'The pole at v = ω/k is the whole story: particles moving at the wave phase velocity exchange energy resonantly with it. Landau\'s prescription for the contour — deform below the pole, as required by causality in the initial-value problem — is what produces the imaginary part. Vlasov had treated it as a principal value and found no damping, which is the error Landau corrected.'},
  {tex:R`\frac{\partial\mathbf{B}}{\partial t}=\nabla\times(\mathbf{u}\times\mathbf{B})+\eta\nabla^{2}\mathbf{B},\qquad R_{m}=\frac{uL}{\eta}`, name:'Induction equation',
   gloss:'A competition between advection and diffusion. R_m ≈ 10⁶ in the solar convection zone and 10¹⁰ in the interstellar medium, so the diffusive term is negligible almost everywhere — and yet it cannot be set to zero, because it is the highest-derivative term. The structure is identical to the Reynolds number problem in fluid mechanics, and the resolution is the same: thin boundary layers.'},
  {tex:R`\rho\frac{d\mathbf{u}}{dt}=-\nabla p+\frac{1}{\mu_{0}}(\nabla\times\mathbf{B})\times\mathbf{B},\qquad \beta=\frac{2\mu_{0}p}{B^{2}}`, name:'MHD momentum equation and plasma beta',
   gloss:'The Lorentz force splits into a magnetic pressure B²/2μ₀ and a tension B²/μ₀ along field lines — which is why the frozen-in field behaves like an elastic medium and why Alfvén waves exist. β compares thermal to magnetic pressure: about 10⁻² to 10⁻⁴ in the solar corona, of order 1 in the solar wind, a few percent in a tokamak, where the achievable β limits the power density of a reactor.'},
  {tex:R`\gamma_{\rm Landau}\propto \left.\frac{\partial f_{0}}{\partial v}\right|_{v=\omega/k}`, name:'Landau damping rate',
   gloss:'The sign follows the slope of the distribution at the phase velocity. A Maxwellian has a negative slope everywhere on the positive axis, so waves damp — slightly slower particles are accelerated and take energy from the wave. Invert the slope with a beam and the same formula gives growth: the bump-on-tail instability. Damping and instability are one expression with two signs.'},
  {tex:R`\tau_{E}\propto I_{p}^{0.93}B^{0.15}P^{-0.69}n^{0.41}M^{0.19}R^{1.97}\varepsilon^{0.58}\kappa^{0.78}`, name:'IPB98(y,2) confinement scaling',
   gloss:'Included deliberately as a quantity that is measured rather than derived. It is a multivariate log-linear regression over a multi-machine database, and it is the basis of ITER\'s performance projection. The negative power of heating power is the notorious part: the harder you heat, the worse the confinement. No first-principles theory reproduces these exponents.'}
 ],
 concepts:[
  {t:'The Debye sphere and the plasma parameter', d:'λ_D is the screening length; Λ = nλ_D³ counts the particles inside a Debye sphere and measures how collective the plasma is. Λ ≈ 10⁸ in a tokamak and ≈ 10⁹ in the solar wind, so the collision term is a small correction; Λ ~ 1 in white dwarf interiors and laser-compressed fuel, where the expansion parameter is of order unity and the standard theory does not apply. The textbook definition of "a plasma" is really the condition Λ ≫ 1, not merely ionisation.'},
  {t:'Flux freezing', d:'In the ideal limit, magnetic flux through any comoving surface is conserved, so field lines are material curves and the field topology cannot change. This underwrites almost every qualitative argument in astrophysical magnetism — field amplification by collapse, angular-momentum transport along field lines, the confinement of cosmic rays. Alfvén, who proved it, spent his later career warning that it was being used in precisely the cases where the ideal Ohm\'s law fails.'},
  {t:'Landau damping', d:'Collisionless damping of a wave by resonant particles, obtained from an equation with no dissipation, no collision term and exact time-reversal symmetry. The correct account is phase mixing: the perturbation develops ever finer structure in velocity, so velocity-space integrals — and hence the electric field — decay while the distribution function itself does not. Nothing is lost, and the plasma echo recovers it. Mouhot and Villani proved nonlinear Landau damping in 2011, showing the phenomenon survives beyond linearisation for analytic data.'},
  {t:'Ideal MHD instabilities', d:'Classified by the energy principle of Bernstein, Frieman, Kruskal and Kulsrud (1958): the equilibrium is stable if δW > 0 for all displacements. The kink (m = 1) sets the Kruskal–Shafranov safety-factor limit q > 1 that governs tokamak current; the sausage (m = 0) destroyed the early pinch programme; interchange and ballooning modes limit achievable β. These are the only plasma predictions that behave like clean theory-driven limits, and they are respected in practice.'},
  {t:'Magnetic reconnection', d:'Topology change in a current sheet where the ideal Ohm\'s law fails over a thin layer. It converts magnetic energy to kinetic and thermal energy, powers flares, coronal mass ejections, magnetospheric substorms and tokamak sawteeth. The rate is the problem: Sweet–Parker gives S^{-1/2}, which is far too slow, and the modern picture — plasmoid cascades above S ≈ 10⁴, Hall physics at the ion skin depth — gives a rate near 0.1 v_A that is largely independent of the microphysics. Why the rate should be universal remains partly open.'},
  {t:'Anomalous transport', d:'The name for the gap between predicted and observed cross-field heat flux in confinement devices — one to two orders of magnitude over classical, about a factor of ten over neoclassical in the electron channel. It is not anomalous in the sense of unexplained; it is turbulent transport driven by drift-wave instabilities. The name has stuck for sixty years and is a small monument to how a field records its own history in its vocabulary.'},
  {t:'Dynamo action', d:'The maintenance of a magnetic field against ohmic decay by fluid motion. Cowling\'s theorem forbids the axisymmetric case, so all real dynamos rely on helical flow and differential rotation. The solar dynamo\'s 22-year cycle is described by mean-field models with turbulent transport coefficients (the α and β effects) that are parameterised rather than computed; global simulations reproduce cyclic behaviour but not reliably at solar parameters.'}
 ],
 results:[
  {n:'Langmuir\'s probe and the naming of the plasma (1928)', s:'Langmuir and Tonks characterised sheath formation and oscillation at ω_pe; the probe method still gives n_e and T_e in laboratory discharges to about 10–20%.'},
  {n:'Measurement of Landau damping (Malmberg & Wharton, 1964)', s:'Exponential spatial decay of a launched wave in a collisionless column, with the damping length following exp(−1/2k²λ_D²) as predicted eighteen years earlier.'},
  {n:'The plasma echo (1967–68)', s:'A wave reappears at a calculable time after two damped waves, demonstrating that Landau damping destroys no information — the cleanest experimental statement about reversibility in any macroscopic system.'},
  {n:'H-mode discovery (ASDEX, 1982)', s:'Wagner and colleagues found a spontaneous transition to roughly doubled energy confinement above a power threshold, with an edge transport barrier. Unpredicted, and the operating regime on which ITER is designed.'},
  {n:'JET deuterium–tritium records', s:'16 MW peak fusion power and Q ≈ 0.67 in 1997; 59 MJ over five seconds in 2021 and 69 MJ in 2023, in the largest tokamak built to date.'},
  {n:'NIF ignition (5 December 2022)', s:'2.05 MJ of laser energy in, 3.15 MJ of fusion energy out — target gain above unity for the first time, with subsequent shots exceeding it. Facility wall-plug efficiency makes the overall energy balance strongly negative.'},
  {n:'MMS reconnection measurements (2015–16)', s:'Four spacecraft at 10 km separation resolved the electron diffusion region at the magnetopause, confirming kinetic simulation predictions in situ.'},
  {n:'The magnetorotational instability (1991)', s:'Balbus and Hawley supplied the mechanism for accretion-disc turbulence, converting Shakura and Sunyaev\'s fitted α into something with a physical basis — though α itself is still fitted in most applications.'}
 ],
 valid:{
  holds:'Λ ≫ 1 and length scales above λ_D for kinetic theory; additionally ω ≪ Ω_ci and L ≫ ρ_i for MHD. In practice this covers the solar corona and wind, the magnetosphere, the interstellar and intracluster medium, tokamaks and stellarators, and most industrial discharges. Ideal-MHD stability limits are respected in devices to within tens of percent.',
  breaks:'Strongly coupled regimes (Λ ≲ 1): white-dwarf and giant-planet interiors, laser-compressed inertial-confinement fuel, dusty plasmas, ultracold neutral plasmas. Sheaths and other non-neutral boundary layers. Any process at the ion gyroradius or ion skin depth — reconnection diffusion regions, collisionless shocks, particle acceleration — where fluid descriptions fail entirely and one must go back to kinetics.'
 },
 detail:{
  deriv:[
   {h:'The ladder of closures, and what each rung throws away', b:'Begin with the Klimontovich distribution, a sum of delta functions following the exact trajectories of every particle. It is exact, reversible, and contains everything, including the complete microstate — and is therefore useless. Ensemble-average it: f = ⟨N⟩, and the equation for f contains a term involving the two-particle correlation, which is O(1/Λ). Drop it and you have Vlasov, still reversible and now tractable. Keep it to leading order and you get the Landau–Fokker–Planck collision operator, which is where irreversibility genuinely enters: it has an H-theorem. Take velocity moments of the kinetic equation and you get fluid equations for each species, unclosed because the nth moment equation contains the (n+1)th; close it with an equation of state or a heat-flux model, and you have two-fluid theory. Add the MHD ordering — slow, large, non-relativistic, quasineutral — and the two fluids combine into one, with the electron momentum equation becoming a generalised Ohm\'s law. Drop every term in that Ohm\'s law except u × B and you have ideal MHD. Five rungs, each with an explicit small parameter, and at each one the information discarded is not recoverable from what remains. What makes this hierarchy philosophically unusual is that the rungs are not nested in accuracy in a simple way: ideal MHD works well in plasmas where the collisionality condition used to derive it is violated by ten orders of magnitude.'},
   {h:'Landau damping, and why the usual account is wrong', b:'Linearise Vlasov about a Maxwellian and Fourier-transform in space. The temptation is to look for normal modes, which is what Vlasov did in 1938; he found a real dispersion relation and no damping. Landau\'s move in 1946 was to treat it as an initial-value problem and Laplace-transform in time. The inversion contour must lie above all singularities for causality; deforming it down to evaluate the long-time behaviour requires passing around the pole at v = ω/k in the velocity integral, and the residue is imaginary. Hence damping. Now, what is physically happening? Not dissipation: the Vlasov equation conserves every entropy functional ∫G(f)d³v d³x exactly. Case and van Kampen showed in the mid-1950s that the linear operator has a continuous spectrum of undamped singular modes, and that Landau damping is the destructive interference of a superposition of them. The perturbation does not decay; it develops structure in velocity at ever finer scale, δf ~ exp(ikvt), so that any smooth velocity-space integral — the charge density, and hence E — decays while the phase-space information persists. The plasma echo makes this observable: the fine structure is still there and can be unwound. The philosophical payoff is precise. Here is macroscopic irreversible-looking behaviour arising from an exactly reversible equation with no coarse-graining, no ensemble, no Stosszahlansatz, and no entropy increase. Whatever explains the arrow of time in this case, it is not the Boltzmannian story.'},
   {h:'Why the ideal limit is singular', b:'Set η = 0 in the induction equation and the highest derivative vanishes. The order of the equation drops, and the solution of the reduced equation cannot satisfy all the boundary and topological conditions that solutions at small η satisfy. Specifically, ideal evolution preserves field topology exactly, while resistive evolution at any η > 0 can change it. Since generic ideal evolution drives current sheets to zero thickness — Parker\'s conjecture, still not fully proved — the neglected term becomes dominant in a layer whose thickness shrinks until it does. The Sweet–Parker layer has δ/L ~ S^{-1/2}, so for S = 10¹² the layer is a millionth of the system size. The structure is exactly Prandtl\'s boundary layer with η in place of μ: the outer ideal solution plus an inner layer where the neglected physics is restored, matched asymptotically. This is why ideal MHD can be quantitatively excellent globally and qualitatively wrong about the one process — reconnection — that determines when the global configuration rearranges.'}
  ],
  deeper:[
   {h:'Landau damping as a test case for theories of irreversibility', b:'The standard accounts of macroscopic irreversibility all involve losing information: coarse-graining a phase-space distribution, taking a thermodynamic limit, imposing a molecular-chaos hypothesis, or appealing to decoherence. Landau damping involves none of these. The Vlasov equation is Hamiltonian in an infinite-dimensional sense, preserves all Casimirs, and has no small parameter that is being averaged over. The observable damps because it is a coarse functional — an integral over velocity — of a fine-structured object. The echo shows the fine structure survives. So the arrow here is in the choice of observable, not in the dynamics and not in an ensemble. This is closer to the notion of "apparent" irreversibility that Bricmont and Lebowitz insist is all there ever is, but it is unusually clean: one can write down the microstate, watch the macro-observable decay, and then explicitly recover it in the laboratory. Mouhot and Villani\'s 2011 proof that the phenomenon persists nonlinearly for analytic initial data — for which Villani shared the Fields Medal — established that it is not an artefact of linearisation, and made precise the sense in which it is a phase-mixing rather than a dissipative phenomenon. Philosophers of statistical mechanics have made surprisingly little use of the case, which is a missed opportunity: it is the one macroscopic relaxation process where the reversibility question has been settled experimentally.'},
   {h:'Confinement scaling: what it means to design a reactor on a regression', b:'ITER\'s central performance claim, Q = 10 at 500 MW, follows from IPB98(y,2), a log-linear fit of energy confinement time to eight engineering variables across a multi-machine database. Its 95% confidence interval on the ITER extrapolation spans roughly a factor of two in τ_E. No first-principles calculation reproduces the exponents, and the strongly negative power-degradation exponent (−0.69) has no accepted derivation. Gyrokinetic simulation can reproduce measured turbulent fluxes in given discharges but cannot yet produce the scaling law. What is being relied on, then, is the assumption that the dimensionless physics is preserved under the extrapolation — that ITER is the same plasma as JET at larger ρ*, which is checked by dedicated dimensionless-similarity experiments and is the real argument. This is empirical scaling used as a predictive instrument well outside the data range, with billions of euros riding on it, and it should interest philosophers of modelling considerably more than it does. It is worth comparing with the Kolmogorov spectrum in hydrodynamics, where a dimensional-analysis prediction also outperforms anything derived from the governing equations — but there the argument is a symmetry argument, and here it is a regression.'}
  ]
 },
 phil:{
  stance:'The clearest worked example of a theory whose entire content lies in its closures rather than its axioms, and which is used successfully outside the regime its derivation licenses. Landau damping should be a standard case in the philosophy of statistical mechanics and is not: it is macroscopic relaxation from an exactly reversible equation, with the lost information experimentally recoverable, and it dissolves the assumption that irreversible appearance requires coarse-graining.',
  disputes:[
   'Whether ideal MHD\'s success in collisionless plasmas is explained by magnetisation substituting for collisions, or is an unexplained empirical fact about which nobody has a derivation.',
   'Whether Landau damping is genuinely irreversible or only apparently so — the Case–van Kampen continuous spectrum and the plasma echo push hard toward "apparently", and the field has largely stopped arguing without the philosophical literature noticing.',
   'Whether fast reconnection is universal because of plasmoid cascades, Hall physics, or turbulence — the observed rate near 0.1 v_A is suspiciously insensitive to which.',
   'Alfvén\'s own objection that the frozen-flux picture, his result, is routinely applied where the ideal Ohm\'s law provably fails.',
   'Whether multi-machine confinement regressions constitute knowledge that licenses extrapolation, or a curve fit whose extrapolation is a bet.'
  ],
  read:['Krall & Trivelpiece, *Principles of Plasma Physics*','Landau, "On the Vibrations of the Electronic Plasma" (1946)','Mouhot & Villani, "On Landau Damping" (2011)','Alfvén, "On Frozen-In Field Lines and Field-Line Reconnection" (1976)','Biskamp, *Magnetic Reconnection in Plasmas*','Ryutov, "Landau Damping: Half a Century with the Great Discovery" (1999)']
 },
 fig:'plasmahier'
},

{
 id:'matsci', n:'Materials Science & Solid Mechanics', aka:'Continuum mechanics · dislocation theory · CALPHAD',
 dom:'physics', tier:4, years:'1660 · 1920 · 1934 – 1956 –', status:'effective',
 scale:{len:'10⁻¹⁰ – 10¹ m', en:'meV – eV', lg:11},
 one:'The theory of how solids deform and break, in which the continuum equations are exact bookkeeping, the material behaviour is supplied by constitutive laws that are measured rather than derived, and the one great triumph — explaining why real metals are a thousand times weaker than their bonds — came from positing a defect that nobody could see for twenty-two years.',
 onto:'A continuous body carrying fields of stress and strain, occupied at a finer scale by a crystal lattice with a population of point, line and planar defects — vacancies, dislocations, grain boundaries, precipitates — whose collective behaviour constitutes what the continuum calls plasticity. The defects are the real subject: a perfect crystal would be almost useless as a structural material, and essentially every property an engineer specifies is a defect property.',
 state:'Continuum: the displacement field u(x,t), from which the strain tensor follows, plus the Cauchy stress tensor σ and a set of internal state variables (dislocation density, damage, hardening) with their own evolution laws. Microstructural: a phase and grain description — volume fractions, orientation distribution, precipitate size spectrum — that is not derivable from the continuum state and is carried alongside it.',

 axioms:[
  {n:'Continuum mechanics is axiomatised; materials science is not',
   s:'The balance laws and constitutive principles below are the Truesdell–Noll axiomatisation of rational continuum mechanics; the microstructural content of materials science has nothing comparable and is a body of mechanisms, correlations and fitted laws.',
   t:'The distinction is the whole subject. Truesdell and Noll\'s *Non-Linear Field Theories of Mechanics* (1965) is a genuine axiomatic system with theorems. It says nothing whatever about why steel yields at 250 MPa. The predictive content of materials science lives entirely in the constitutive functions the axioms leave open.'},
  {n:'The continuum hypothesis',
   s:R`\rho(\mathbf{x})=\lim_{V\to V_{*}}\frac{\Delta m}{\Delta V},\qquad a\ll \ell_{*}\ll L`,
   t:'Fields are defined by limits taken to a volume small compared with the body and large compared with the lattice spacing — a limit that does not exist mathematically and is a physical stipulation. It fails when any of the three scales collide: in thin films a few atoms thick, at crack tips where the stress field is singular, and in nanocrystalline metals where grain size approaches dislocation core size.'},
  {n:'Cauchy\'s stress principle and the existence of the stress tensor',
   s:R`\mathbf{t}(\mathbf{n})=\sigma\mathbf{n};\qquad \sigma_{ij}=\sigma_{ji}\ \text{(from angular momentum balance)}`,
   t:'Cauchy\'s tetrahedron argument (1822) proves that the traction on any surface depends linearly on its normal — so nine numbers, reduced to six by symmetry, capture the entire internal force state at a point. It is a theorem, given the balance laws and a continuity assumption, and it is the reason a vector field is not enough and a full tensor is.'},
  {n:'The balance laws',
   s:R`\dot\rho+\rho\nabla\!\cdot\!\mathbf{v}=0,\quad \rho\dot{\mathbf{v}}=\nabla\!\cdot\!\sigma+\rho\mathbf{b},\quad \rho\dot e=\sigma\!:\!\mathbf{D}-\nabla\!\cdot\!\mathbf{q},\quad \rho\dot\eta\ge-\nabla\!\cdot\!(\mathbf{q}/T)`,
   t:'Mass, momentum, energy and the Clausius–Duhem inequality. They hold for every material and therefore determine none: four equations in far more unknowns. Everything that distinguishes rubber from tungsten is in the constitutive closure, which is why the axiomatic layer is complete and empty at the same time.'},
  {n:'Constitutive principles: determinism, local action, material frame-indifference',
   s:R`\sigma(t)=\mathcal{F}\big[\mathbf{F}(t-s)\big]_{s\ge0},\qquad \mathcal{F}[\mathbf{QF}]=\mathbf{Q}\,\mathcal{F}[\mathbf{F}]\,\mathbf{Q}^{T}`,
   t:'Stress depends on the history of local deformation only, and the response cannot depend on the observer\'s rigid motion. Frame-indifference is a strong restriction that eliminates most naively written constitutive models. It has been questioned — Müller and others noted that molecular derivations of viscosity give small frame-dependent terms — but as an engineering principle it is unchallenged.'},
  {n:'The representative volume element',
   s:R`d_{\rm grain}\ll L_{\rm RVE}\ll L_{\rm macro};\qquad \bar\sigma=\frac{1}{V}\int_{V}\sigma\,dV`,
   t:'Hill (1963): a volume large enough that its average response is independent of size and boundary conditions, yet small enough to be a material point. For a polycrystal this needs 10²–10³ grains, so L_RVE is tens to hundreds of micrometres. Thin sheets, MEMS devices and additively manufactured parts with columnar grains routinely violate it, which is why their properties are size-dependent and why "the yield strength of this alloy" can fail to be well defined.'},
  {n:'Small-strain kinematics for linear elasticity',
   s:R`\varepsilon_{ij}=\tfrac12(\partial_{i}u_{j}+\partial_{j}u_{i}),\qquad \sigma_{ij}=C_{ijkl}\varepsilon_{kl}`,
   t:'Hooke\'s law generalised, with C having 21 independent components for a triclinic crystal, three for cubic and two for an isotropic body. It is the first term of a Taylor expansion of the interatomic potential about equilibrium and is accurate to strains of a few tenths of a percent in metals — which is to say, for the entire elastic range and nothing beyond it.'},
  {n:'Local thermodynamic equilibrium of phases, where phase equilibria are invoked',
   s:R`F=C-P+2\ \ \text{(Gibbs phase rule)};\qquad \mu_{i}^{\alpha}=\mu_{i}^{\beta}\ \text{at equilibrium}`,
   t:'Phase diagrams are equilibrium objects. Most engineering materials are not at equilibrium — martensite, precipitation-hardened alloys, quenched steels and every glass are metastable by design. The phase diagram therefore tells you what the material is trying to become, not what it is, and the distinction is the basis of the entire heat-treatment industry.'}
 ],

 hyps:[
  {h:'Plastic yield in crystals occurs by the glide of dislocations, not by rigid slip of whole planes.',
   k:'core',
   b:'Posited independently by Orowan, Polanyi and Taylor in 1934 to close a factor of 10³–10⁴ between the theoretical shear strength G/2π and observed yield strengths. Nobody saw a dislocation until Hirsch, Horne and Whelan imaged them moving in aluminium by transmission electron microscopy in 1956. For twenty-two years the central entity of the theory was a posit justified solely by the magnitude it explained — a clean historical case for scientific realism to chew on, and one it rarely uses.'},
  {h:'Grain boundaries obstruct dislocation motion, giving strength proportional to d^{−1/2}.',
   k:'auxiliary',
   b:'Hall (1951) and Petch (1953). The scaling is robust over three decades of grain size; the mechanism is not settled — pile-up models, grain-boundary source models and strain-gradient arguments all produce the same exponent from different physics. It is revisable without touching dislocation theory itself, and it is revised: below roughly 10–30 nm the trend inverts as grain-boundary sliding takes over.'},
  {h:'Fracture is controlled by the energy released when a pre-existing flaw extends, not by the local stress reaching a bond-breaking value.',
   k:'core',
   b:'Griffith, 1920–21, from experiments on glass fibres whose strength rose as diameter fell. The energy criterion explains why strength is statistical and size-dependent and why a scratch is catastrophic. Irwin and Orowan later added the plastic work term, which for metals is 10²–10⁴ times the surface energy — so the original derivation is correct in form and wrong by orders of magnitude in the value of the constant, for exactly the reason Griffith excluded.'},
  {h:'Elastic constants, surface energies and stacking-fault energies can be computed from electronic structure.',
   k:'bridge',
   b:'Density functional theory gives elastic constants of simple metals to 5–10% and stacking-fault energies to a few tens of mJ/m², which feed dislocation models as inputs. This is the one genuine quantitative bridge from quantum mechanics to mechanical behaviour, and it reaches the elastic and defect-energy level only. No electronic-structure calculation predicts a yield strength.'},
  {h:'Alloy phase stability can be computed by fitting Gibbs energy functions to measured data under thermodynamic constraints.',
   k:'auxiliary',
   b:'The CALPHAD method (Kaufman and Bernstein, 1970): represent each phase\'s Gibbs energy as a Redlich–Kister polynomial with coefficients fitted to thermochemical and phase-boundary measurements, then compute multicomponent equilibria by minimisation. It is an assessed interpolation, constrained by thermodynamics, not a calculation from below. It works well inside the assessed composition range and extrapolates unreliably outside it.'},
  {h:'Steels possess a true fatigue limit: below some stress amplitude they never fail.',
   k:'abandoned',
   b:'Standard design practice through most of the twentieth century, based on S–N curves that flatten near 10⁶–10⁷ cycles. Very-high-cycle testing with ultrasonic machines out to 10⁹–10¹⁰ cycles found failures below the supposed limit, initiating at interior inclusions and producing the characteristic "fish-eye" fracture surface. The limit was an artefact of test duration. It has been replaced by a duplex S–N description with no asymptote.'},
  {h:'The theoretical strength of a crystal is G/2π, set by the shear stress needed to slide one atomic plane over another.',
   k:'abandoned',
   b:'Frenkel, 1926 — a sinusoidal estimate of the lattice resistance, giving a few GPa for typical metals. It is right about perfect crystals and wrong about real ones by three to four orders of magnitude. It was not discarded but demoted: it survives as the upper bound that whiskers approach (Herring and Galt, 1952, measured tin whiskers near it) and as the quantity the dislocation hypothesis exists to explain away.'}
 ],

 preds:[
  {p:'A perfect crystal should shear at about a tenth of its shear modulus.',
   num:R`\tau_{\rm th}\approx G/2\pi\ (\text{Frenkel});\ \text{observed } \tau_{y}\sim G/10^{4}\text{–}G/10^{5}`,
   st:'failed',
   b:'Frenkel\'s 1926 estimate compared with annealed copper (τ_y ≈ 1 MPa against G ≈ 48 GPa) is wrong by roughly four orders of magnitude. What was done about it is the interesting part: rather than abandon the lattice picture, three people independently posited an unobserved line defect that lets slip proceed one row at a time. The failed prediction is the origin of the field\'s central concept.'},
  {p:'Defect-free crystals — whiskers — should approach the theoretical strength.',
   num:R`\text{tin whiskers: strengths approaching}\ \sim G/30`,
   st:'confirmed',
   b:'Herring and Galt, 1952, measured elastic bending of micrometre-diameter tin whiskers to strains of a few percent and strengths orders of magnitude above bulk tin. This is the control experiment for the dislocation hypothesis: remove the dislocations and the discrepancy disappears. Modern micropillar compression reproduces the effect and shows a smaller-is-stronger size dependence over 0.1–10 μm.'},
  {p:'Line defects of the posited geometry exist and move under stress.',
   num:R`b\approx0.25\ \mathrm{nm};\ \rho_{\rm disl}\sim10^{10}\ \mathrm{m^{-2}}\ (\text{annealed})\to10^{15}\ \mathrm{m^{-2}}\ (\text{cold-worked})`,
   st:'confirmed',
   b:'Hirsch, Horne and Whelan at Cambridge in 1956 imaged dislocations in thin aluminium foils by diffraction contrast and filmed them moving and multiplying under beam heating; Bollmann obtained similar images independently. Twenty-two years after the posit. Measured densities and Burgers vectors match what the 1934 theory required, and Frank–Read multiplication sources were subsequently imaged as predicted.'},
  {p:'Fracture strength scales as the inverse square root of flaw size.',
   num:R`\sigma_{f}=\sqrt{\frac{2E\gamma_{s}}{\pi a}}`,
   st:'confirmed',
   b:'Griffith tested glass fibres and bulbs with deliberately introduced cracks in 1920 and found σ√a constant to about 10%. For brittle solids with known γ_s the absolute value is right. For metals it is wrong by 10²–10⁴ because plastic work at the crack tip dwarfs surface energy — Irwin and Orowan\'s correction, which preserves the scaling and replaces the constant with a measured toughness.'},
  {p:'Yield strength rises as the inverse square root of grain size, without limit.',
   num:R`\sigma_{y}=\sigma_{0}+k_{y}d^{-1/2};\ \text{inverts below } d\approx10\text{–}30\ \mathrm{nm}`,
   st:'failed',
   b:'The relation holds from millimetre grains down to about 20 nm and then reverses — softening with further refinement, seen in nanocrystalline copper and nickel and reproduced by molecular dynamics (Schiøtz and colleagues, 1998). Below that size there is no room for a pile-up and deformation shifts to grain-boundary sliding. A scaling law with a mechanism-change at its end, not a universal law.'},
  {p:'CALPHAD-assessed databases predict which multicomponent alloys form a single solid solution.',
   num:R`—`,
   st:'failed',
   b:'The high-entropy alloy programme after 2004 assumed configurational entropy would stabilise single-phase solid solutions in five-component equimolar systems. Systematic surveys found that the large majority of such compositions are multiphase, and the entropy argument was too crude. CALPHAD extrapolation into the unassessed centre of a five-component composition space is exactly where it is least reliable, and the field learned this expensively.'},
  {p:'Fatigue crack growth follows a power law in the stress-intensity range.',
   num:R`da/dN=C(\Delta K)^{m},\ m\approx3\ \text{for steels}`,
   st:'retrodiction',
   b:'Paris proposed the correlation in 1961 after the data existed; it was fitted to observed growth curves, not predicted. It is a superb interpolation in the mid-range regime and has no derivation from crack-tip mechanics; C and m are material constants determined by testing. Essentially all damage-tolerant design in aerospace rests on it, which is worth stating plainly: the airworthiness of a fleet depends on a fitted power law.'},
  {p:'Elastic constants computed from density functional theory match measurement.',
   num:R`\text{typically } 5\text{–}10\%\ \text{for simple metals}`,
   st:'confirmed',
   b:'Consistently verified across the periodic table since the 1980s. This is the bridge hypothesis doing real work, and it marks the boundary precisely: quantum mechanics predicts the elastic response, which is a property of the perfect lattice, and predicts essentially nothing about yield, toughness or fatigue, which are properties of defect populations and their history.'}
 ],

 eqs:[
  {tex:R`\sigma_{ij}=C_{ijkl}\varepsilon_{kl},\qquad \varepsilon_{ij}=\tfrac12(u_{i,j}+u_{j,i})`, name:'Generalised Hooke\'s law',
   gloss:'Stress is linear in strain through a fourth-rank tensor with at most 21 independent components. Note that the strain tensor is a symmetrised gradient, so it discards the rotational part of the deformation — this is why a rigid rotation produces no stress and why the small-strain measure fails for large rotations, requiring the Green–Lagrange measure instead.'},
  {tex:R`\tau_{\rm th}\approx \frac{G}{2\pi}\quad\text{vs}\quad \tau_{y}\sim \frac{G}{10^{4}}\ \text{–}\ \frac{G}{10^{5}}`, name:'The strength gap',
   gloss:'The central quantitative fact of the subject. For copper, G = 48 GPa gives a theoretical shear strength near 7 GPa; annealed polycrystalline copper yields near 1–10 MPa. Three to four orders of magnitude, and the whole of dislocation theory exists to account for it. Very few disciplines have so sharp a founding discrepancy.'},
  {tex:R`\tau=\frac{Gb}{2\pi(1-\nu)}\exp\!\left[-\frac{2\pi w}{b}\right],\qquad w=\frac{d}{1-\nu}`, name:'Peierls–Nabarro stress',
   gloss:'The lattice friction opposing dislocation glide, exponentially sensitive to the ratio of core width to Burgers vector. Wide cores in face-centred cubic metals give a tiny Peierls stress, so they are ductile at all temperatures; narrow cores in body-centred cubic metals and covalent crystals give a large and strongly temperature-dependent one, which is why steel has a ductile-to-brittle transition and copper does not.'},
  {tex:R`\sigma_{y}=\sigma_{0}+k_{y}d^{-1/2}`, name:'Hall–Petch relation',
   gloss:'An empirical law with several competing derivations, all giving the same exponent. k_y ranges from about 0.1 MPa·m^{1/2} in aluminium to 0.6 in low-carbon steel. Grain refinement is the only strengthening mechanism that raises strength without sacrificing toughness, which is why it dominates modern steel processing — and it stops working below about 20 nm.'},
  {tex:R`\sigma_{f}=\sqrt{\frac{2E\gamma_{\rm eff}}{\pi a}},\qquad K_{I}=Y\sigma\sqrt{\pi a},\qquad K_{IC}\ \text{measured}`, name:'Griffith criterion and fracture toughness',
   gloss:'The Griffith form is a derivation; K_IC is a measured material property, standardised in ASTM E399 with prescribed specimen geometry. Values: about 0.7 MPa√m for soda-lime glass, 20–40 for aerospace aluminium alloys, 50–150 for structural steels. Note the honesty of the standard — it specifies the test, because the quantity is defined by the test.'},
  {tex:R`\frac{da}{dN}=C(\Delta K)^{m}`, name:'Paris law',
   gloss:'A fitted power law for fatigue crack growth with no derivation from crack-tip mechanics, with m ≈ 3 for steels and 3–4 for aluminium alloys. Included here as a quantity that is measured rather than derived, and as an example of a correlation on which safety-critical design genuinely depends. Integrating it gives inspection intervals for airframes.'},
  {tex:R`G^{\phi}=\sum_{i}x_{i}{}^{\circ}G_{i}^{\phi}+RT\sum_{i}x_{i}\ln x_{i}+\sum_{i<j}x_{i}x_{j}\sum_{\nu}L_{ij}^{\nu}(x_{i}-x_{j})^{\nu}`, name:'CALPHAD Gibbs energy of a solution phase',
   gloss:'Reference term, ideal mixing term, and a Redlich–Kister excess series whose interaction coefficients L are fitted to measured phase boundaries and calorimetry. Equilibrium is then computed by global minimisation over phases. The thermodynamics is exact; the input functions are assessments. The distinction is routinely blurred when results are described as "calculated".'}
 ],
 concepts:[
  {t:'The representative volume element', d:'The construct that makes "a material point" meaningful: a volume containing enough microstructure that its average properties are size-independent, but small enough to be treated as a point. For polycrystals this needs of order a thousand grains. When a component is only a few grains thick — a stent strut, a MEMS beam, a turbine blade with columnar solidification — no RVE exists, the continuum fields are not well defined, and measured properties become specimen-dependent. Most of the "size effects" literature is about this failure.'},
  {t:'Dislocations', d:'Line defects characterised by a Burgers vector, of order 0.25 nm. Edge dislocations glide by breaking one row of bonds at a time, which is why the required stress is thousands of times below rigid slip. Densities range from 10¹⁰ m⁻² annealed to 10¹⁵ m⁻² cold-worked — the latter is 10⁹ km of dislocation line in a cubic centimetre. Work hardening is dislocations obstructing each other; annealing is their removal.'},
  {t:'Strengthening mechanisms', d:'Four, and they are additive to first order: grain refinement (Hall–Petch), solid solution, precipitation or dispersion (Orowan bowing between particles), and work hardening (Taylor, σ ∝ Gb√ρ). Each obstructs dislocation motion by a different mechanism. All four trade against ductility except the first, which is why grain refinement is prized and why the strength–toughness trade-off is the central design constraint in structural alloys.'},
  {t:'Phase diagrams and metastability', d:'Equilibrium maps of which phases coexist at given composition and temperature, constrained by the Gibbs phase rule. Their practical use is almost entirely about departures from them: martensite is a diffusionless product of quenching that appears nowhere on the equilibrium diagram, and precipitation hardening works by arresting a system partway to equilibrium. The diagram supplies the driving force; the kinetics supply the material.'},
  {t:'The fitted nature of engineering properties', d:'Yield strength, ultimate tensile strength, elongation, fracture toughness, fatigue limit, creep rate — none of these is computed from below for any real alloy. They are measured by standardised tests, tabulated, and fitted to composition and processing by regression or, increasingly, by machine learning on the same tabulations. The Materials Genome Initiative of 2011 was an explicit institutional admission of this, and its progress has been real at the level of screening and slight at the level of mechanical properties.'},
  {t:'Multiscale modelling and its seams', d:'The standard chain runs DFT → interatomic potentials → molecular dynamics → discrete dislocation dynamics → crystal plasticity → continuum FEM. Each step passes parameters upward. The seams are where the trouble is: MD runs at strain rates of 10⁷ s⁻¹ or faster against laboratory rates of 10⁻³ s⁻¹, a gap of ten orders of magnitude bridged by assumption, and dislocation dynamics simulations reach microseconds and micrometres. The chain is a pipeline of calibrations, not a derivation.'},
  {t:'Ashby maps', d:'Log–log plots of one property against another across all material classes, with envelopes for each family and guidelines of constant performance index. They make material selection a geometric operation and are the most useful synthetic tool the field has. They are also purely empirical: every point is a measurement, and the structure visible in them — the clustering of families, the empty regions — is a summary of what has been made, not of what is possible.'}
 ],
 results:[
  {n:'Dislocation hypothesis (1934) and imaging (1956)', s:'Orowan, Polanyi and Taylor independently; Hirsch, Horne and Whelan imaged them by TEM twenty-two years later, closing a 10³–10⁴ discrepancy in yield strength.'},
  {n:'Griffith fracture theory (1920)', s:'σ√a constant to about 10% in glass fibres, with strength rising as fibre diameter falls — the origin of statistical, size-dependent strength and of fibre-reinforced composites.'},
  {n:'Hall–Petch strengthening', s:'k_y ≈ 0.6 MPa·m^{1/2} in low-carbon steel; grain refinement from 100 μm to 1 μm roughly triples yield strength without loss of toughness. Inverts below about 20 nm.'},
  {n:'Whisker strength (Herring & Galt, 1952)', s:'Micrometre tin whiskers elastically strained by several percent, approaching theoretical strength — the control that established defects as the cause of weakness.'},
  {n:'CALPHAD databases', s:'Multicomponent equilibria for steels, superalloys and solders computed routinely from assessed Gibbs energies; commercial databases now cover 30+ elements and are standard in alloy design.'},
  {n:'Fracture mechanics in service', s:'Irwin\'s stress-intensity formulation and the Paris law underpin damage-tolerant design; they were driven into practice by the Comet crashes of 1954 and the Liberty ship failures of the 1940s.'},
  {n:'Nanoindentation and micropillar testing', s:'Mechanical properties measured at 10⁻⁷ m scale, revealing smaller-is-stronger behaviour and intermittent, avalanche-like plastic flow that the smooth continuum stress–strain curve averages away.'}
 ],
 valid:{
  holds:'Bodies large compared with the RVE — typically above ~100 μm for polycrystals — at strain rates of 10⁻⁶ to 10² s⁻¹ and temperatures below about 0.4 of the melting point, where linear elasticity and rate-independent plasticity apply. Finite-element stress analysis in this regime is accurate to a few percent given correct constitutive data.',
  breaks:'Below the RVE scale, where properties become specimen-dependent and plastic flow is intermittent; at crack tips, where the continuum stress field diverges as r^{−1/2} and the theory requires a fitted process zone; at high homologous temperature, where creep and diffusion dominate and constitutive laws are empirical fits (Norton, Larson–Miller); and for any property involving defect populations, which no first-principles calculation reaches.'
 },
 detail:{
  deriv:[
   {h:'The strength gap and how it was closed', b:'Frenkel\'s 1926 estimate: model the shear resistance between two atomic planes as sinusoidal in displacement with period b, amplitude set by requiring the initial slope to equal G. The peak stress is then τ_th = Gb/2πd ≈ G/2π, a few gigapascals for a typical metal. Measured yield strengths of annealed pure metals are 10⁶–10⁷ Pa. Three to four orders of magnitude. In 1934 Orowan, Polanyi and Taylor — all three within months, in different countries, without collaboration — proposed that slip does not occur rigidly but by the propagation of a line defect, so that only the bonds along the dislocation line are broken at any instant. The required stress drops to the Peierls–Nabarro value, which is exponentially small in the core width and comes out in the right range for face-centred cubic metals. The hypothesis was accepted long before any dislocation was observed, on the strength of the magnitude it explained and of subsequent quantitative successes: the Taylor relation σ ∝ Gb√ρ for work hardening, the Frank–Read multiplication mechanism, the prediction that defect-free whiskers should be near-theoretically strong. Herring and Galt confirmed the last in 1952; Hirsch, Horne and Whelan imaged the defect itself in 1956. Twenty-two years of successful theorising about an unobserved entity, followed by observation. It is a better case study for the realism debate than most of the ones actually used, because the posit was quantitative, risky, and confirmed by a technique nobody anticipated.'},
   {h:'Why yield strength is not calculated from electronic structure', b:'Follow the chain and see where it breaks. Density functional theory gives the cohesive energy, lattice constant and elastic constants of a pure metal to within about 5%, and stacking-fault energies to a few tens of mJ/m². These are properties of the perfect or nearly perfect lattice, and DFT is genuinely predictive for them. Yield, however, is the stress at which a *population* of pre-existing dislocations begins to move and multiply against a *population* of obstacles. It depends on the dislocation density, the grain size distribution, the precipitate size and spacing, the solute distribution, and the entire thermomechanical history of the specimen. None of these is a ground-state property; all are contingent on processing. Even given the microstructure, the collective dynamics is an avalanche problem with long-range elastic interactions and no small parameter. Discrete dislocation dynamics can simulate about 10⁻⁵ s in a volume of about 10 μm³, which is far from a tensile test. So the honest statement is that the mechanism of yield is understood and the value of the yield strength is measured. Textbook narratives that present materials science as quantum mechanics worked upward are describing an aspiration.'},
   {h:'What a tensile test actually reports', b:'A specimen of standardised geometry is pulled at constant crosshead rate; load is measured by a cell, extension by an extensometer clipped over a defined gauge length, typically 25 or 50 mm. Engineering stress is load divided by *original* area and engineering strain is extension divided by *original* gauge length, so both are wrong once the specimen has deformed appreciably, and the reported ultimate tensile strength is an artefact of that convention — the true stress rises monotonically to fracture, while the engineering curve turns over at the onset of necking. Yield strength is not a measured threshold either: for materials without a sharp yield point it is defined as the 0.2% offset proof stress, a convention. Elongation at fracture depends on the gauge length chosen, because necking localises. Every number in a materials datasheet is therefore a standardised construct, and the standards (ASTM E8, ISO 6892) exist because the quantities do not exist independently of a measurement protocol. This is operationalism as engineering practice rather than as philosophy, and it works.'}
  ],
  deeper:[
   {h:'A mature science whose central quantities are not derived', b:'Materials science sits below chemistry and above engineering in the usual ordering, and is expected on that picture to derive its content from condensed matter physics. It does not. Band theory explains why copper conducts and diamond does not, and says nothing about why copper work-hardens. The concepts that carry the explanatory weight — dislocation, grain boundary, precipitate, RVE, toughness — are defined at their own scale and have no counterparts below. Some are not even well defined below: a grain boundary is a two-dimensional object in a description that has no two-dimensional objects. This is not a temporary state of ignorance awaiting more computing power, because the quantities are historical: the yield strength of a piece of steel is a fact about what was done to it, and no ground-state calculation encodes a processing history. The right philosophical description is something like Wimsatt\'s mechanisms or Cartwright\'s dappled world, with the important addition that the field is entirely candid about it. Engineering handbooks do not claim to derive their tables. The interesting question is why a discipline with this structure is nonetheless cumulative and reliable, and the answer seems to be standardisation: shared test protocols make measurements transferable even when the quantities measured have no theoretical derivation.'},
   {h:'CALPHAD: thermodynamics as a constraint on interpolation', b:'A CALPHAD calculation is often reported as "the calculated phase diagram", which invites the reading that thermodynamics plus composition yields the diagram. What actually happens: each phase is assigned a Gibbs energy expression — a reference term, an ideal-mixing term, and a Redlich–Kister excess polynomial — whose coefficients are fitted to measured phase boundaries, calorimetry, activity measurements and, increasingly, DFT-computed formation energies of end members. The fitting is done by weighted least squares over heterogeneous data, and the result is an assessment. Equilibrium is then computed by minimising total Gibbs energy over all phases, which is exact given the inputs. So the thermodynamic structure is genuine and does real work: it enforces consistency between independent measurements, allows extrapolation from binary and ternary assessments into higher-order systems, and rules out combinations of data. But the content is measured. The high-entropy alloy episode after 2004 exposed the limit: extrapolating assessments into the unexplored centre of five-component composition space predicted single-phase solid solutions that mostly turned out to be multiphase. The lesson is general and applies well beyond materials: a framework that enforces consistency among measurements can look like a predictive theory for as long as one stays inside the region where the measurements were made.'}
  ]
 },
 phil:{
  stance:'The discipline that most clearly separates understanding a mechanism from computing a quantity. Dislocation theory explains yield completely and predicts no yield strength; CALPHAD enforces thermodynamic consistency and derives no phase diagram; fracture mechanics supplies the form of the criterion and measures its constant. That a field can be this reliable, this cumulative and this useful while deriving almost nothing from below is a standing datum against the layered-reduction picture, and it is a datum the field states openly rather than concealing.',
  disputes:[
   'Whether the Hall–Petch exponent reflects dislocation pile-up, grain-boundary sources, or strain-gradient plasticity — three derivations, one exponent, and no experiment that distinguishes them cleanly.',
   'Whether the smaller-is-stronger size effect in micropillars is a dislocation-starvation phenomenon or an artefact of source truncation and specimen preparation.',
   'Whether multiscale modelling chains constitute derivation or a sequence of calibrations, given the ten-order-of-magnitude strain-rate gap between molecular dynamics and laboratory testing.',
   'Whether material frame-indifference is a principle of physics or a modelling convention, following Müller\'s observation that kinetic-theory derivations produce small frame-dependent terms.',
   'Whether machine-learned interatomic potentials and property regressions represent progress in understanding or a formalisation of the field\'s existing reliance on fitting.'
  ],
  read:['Hull & Bacon, *Introduction to Dislocations*','Ashby, *Materials Selection in Mechanical Design*','Truesdell & Noll, *The Non-Linear Field Theories of Mechanics*','Griffith, "The Phenomena of Rupture and Flow in Solids" (1921)','Lukas, Fries & Sundman, *Computational Thermodynamics: The Calphad Method*','Bensaude-Vincent, "The Construction of a Discipline: Materials Science in the United States" (2001)']
 },
 fig:'strengthgap'
}
];
